\section{Introduction} The energy transition in the built environment is increasingly hindered by grid congestion. At the same time, the sustainability focus is shifting from energy efficiency to material usage, due to its impact on CO$_2$ emissions. Regarding climate goals, the Paris Agreement states that emissions need to be reduced by 45\% by 2030 and reach net zero by 2050 (United Nations, n.d.).
\\

\noindent Transitioning to a net-zero world is one of the biggest challenges of the century. This requires a complete transformation of the energy system, especially the electricity sector. Replacing fossil-fuel-based electricity and heating with renewable energy introduces a fundamental challenge: renewable generation is variable, while electricity demand is not (IEA, 2022). Moreover, providing the built environment with enough electricity to power building installations like electric heat pumps requires more infrastructure than exists. Updating the existing infrastructure to handle the increased demand for electricity power costs hundreds of billions and involves prolonged lead times, insinuating the importance of other innovations that help alleviate the mismatch between supply and demand responsible for grid congestion (\textit{TNO}, n.d.).
\\

\noindent According to Tsekeris et al. (2024), harnessing demand response in electricity, is critical not only for reducing reliance on carbon-intensive peak generation but also for mitigating grid congestion. Demand response refers to the ability to temporarily lower or shift energy consumption to times when supply is more abundant. By reallocating rather than reducing end-use services, demand response maintains user comfort while improving overall system efficiency and resource utilization.
\\

\noindent Demand response in buildings can be achieved through thermal buffering, which offers potential to spread energy generation over time, in some cases even enable seasonal storage, and reduce peak loads on the grid. By decoupling thermal demand from electrical supply, thermal energy storage enables buildings to operate within limited infrastructure capacities and maximize the self-consumption of volatile renewable energy. “\textit{Thermal Buffering refers to the capacity of a material, structure, or environment to absorb, store, and slowly release heat energy, thereby moderating temperature fluctuations over time. This process is crucial for stabilizing internal temperatures and reducing peak energy demand in buildings}” (\textit{Thermal Buffering}, 2025).

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Problem Statement}

Current literature demonstrates the technical potential of Thermal Energy Storage (TES), their application possibilities, thermodynamic capabilities, and their ability to shift grid demand load. However, the literature lacks in holistic design approaches as well as design implementation workflows from start to finish. Existing Building Energy Modelling (BEM) studies focus mainly on TES sizing in the design phase using deterministic, and sometimes static, assumptions. This approach overlooks the dynamic operational complexities required to manage supply-demand fluctuations in modern grids and operational schedules (De Wilde, 2014). Resulting TES systems are often sized and operated sub-optimally, limiting the true benefit of flexible and efficient operation. Moreover, literature has shown that most TES implementation approaches are very case-specific, lacking a more generalized workflow that is applicable to different buildings or building types. 
\\

\noindent Furthermore, while advanced control methods exists, there is lack of validated frameworks that directly integrate the complexities of Building Energy Models (BEM) models with dynamic system control strategies. In order to get more grip on the chosen TES system’s performance regarding its dual objective: maximal grid congestion relief and sustained thermal comfort, this thesis directly addresses the current gap in the literature by establishing and validating a complete simulation-to-control pipeline.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Objective and Research Questions} The aim of this research is to analyse to what extent thermal buffering can be an effective and sustainable solution to grid congestion. The design of a workflow for TES system integration into building design is central in this research. Furthermore, a simulation model with an optimization framework explores optimal TES system sizing and control strategy of thermal buffers to guarantee maximum peak load shifting and ensuring sustained occupant thermal comfort.
\\

\noindent Hence, the main research question: \textit{How can a design-to-operation framework facilitate the co-optimization of Thermal Energy Storage (TES) capacity and control strategies to enhance load-shifting potential while guaranteeing occupant thermal comfort in buildings?}
\\

\noindent Since this research paper aims to develop a TES integration and control framework, the main sub-question are formulated as:  
\begin{enumerate}
    \item How do specific building load curves and grid congestion windows dictate the selection of a suitable TES technology?
    \item What are the primary infrastructure and system-level requirements for integrating TES into building climate systems to ensure alignment with both spatial constraints and dynamic operational performance?
    \item How can coordinated control strategies integrate active TES and passive building thermal mass to minimize total storage volume while maximizing load-shifting performance and maintaining occupant thermal comfort?
    \item How does the use of dynamic simulation modelling improve the reliability of load-shifting predictions compared to simplified static sizing methods?
    \item To what extent does the application of the design-to-operation framework to a case study achieve the necessary peak-load shifting to meet local grid constraints?
\end{enumerate}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Definition and Scope}The main research question of this paper focusses on a general TES implementation framework. The general framework will then be applied to a case study: The Broedplaats Performing Arts Center in Breda, the Netherlands. Even though the general framework does not prioritize a specific TES system, the second part of this research will focus in depth on the performance and integration of stratified water tanks combined with air-to-water heat pumps. TES systems combined with heat pumps represent one of the most relevant and widely applicable configurations for low-carbon thermal systems, and suitable for a broad variety of building typologies. 

\subsubsection{Boundary Conditions} 

\subsubsection*{General Framework}

\begin{itemize}
    \item The framework can be used as a basis for thermal buffering rather then a precise implementation manual for every TES system;
    \item The methodology focuses on energy aggregation \& delivery and grid-load shifting, prioritizing global system performance over localized, high-resolution thermal delivery modelling;
    \item The framework is structured for system-level design rather than detailed equipment specification, identifying universal optimization drivers while allowing for project-specific constraints.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Case Study}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Short term sensible thermal energy storage;
    \item Solely focussing on space heating and cooling, not DHW (Domestic Hot Water);
    \item Generation system: air-source heat pump, calculated with theoretical COP performance within the python simulation model;
    \item PV integration is included in the simulation model;
    \item Focus on performance based on the extreme climate dataset for the Netherlands from the NEN 5060-B2 1\%.
\end{itemize}

