\subsection{Relevance}

\subsubsection*{Scientific Relevance} This thesis addresses the lack of general integration workflows for thermal energy storage systems in buildings by developing a design-to-operation framework that connects TES selection and sizing with advanced operational control. Additionally, dynamic simulation-based validation is part of this framework. 
\\

\noindent Literature states that TES integration is complex because neither storage technologies nor the processes they serve are "one-size-fits-all". Each application involves unique energy sources, sinks, and factors that are interdependent. To manage this complexity and ensure an optimal match, it is essential to characterize the specific requirements of the mechanisms and capabilities of TES systems independently before attempting to integrate them into a unified application (Gibb et al., 2018). Moreover, the lack of coordination between building design and the design of the energy storage results in systems that functioned as isolated "add-ons", sized merely to meet existing loads rather than being developed as integrated components of a holistic climate strategy (IEA, z.d.). 
\\

\noindent More reasons for underutilized TES applications are mentioned in the research paper of Ayalew en Andrzejczyk (2025), \textit{ Recent Advancements in Latent Thermal Energy Storage and Their Applications for HVAC Systems in Commercial and Residential Buildings in Europe—Analysis of Different EU Countries’ Scenarios}, stating that fragmented (EU) building standards and insufficient training are among the barriers for TES adoption.
\\

\noindent This study investigates how coordinated design and operational decisions can enable optimized charging and discharging strategies that minimize peak electrical demand while maintaining occupant thermal comfort under variable loads. The proposed approach advances the current state of knowledge by demonstrating how coordinated design and control decisions can enhance system-level performance and support grid-responsive building operation. In doing so, this research seeks to bridge the gap between the theoretical potential of thermal energy storage and its practical implementation in building energy systems. 

\subsubsection*{Societal Relevance} The electrification of heating systems in buildings is expected to accelerate as a result of climate targets, increasing renewable electricity generation, and the gradual phase-out of fossil-fuel based heating technologies. Heat pumps represent a key solution in this transition due to their high efficiency and low \(\mathrm{CO_2}\) emissions compared to conventional heating systems. However, widespread adoption of electrically driven heat pumps may significantly increase peak electricity demand, thereby placing additional stress on local distribution grids and potentially increasing the use of carbon-intensive peak generation. In short, the transition to sustainable energy sources to mitigate fossil-fuel dependence introduces a critical counter-effect: the exacerbation of electrical grid congestion.
\\

\noindent Thermal Energy Storage (TES) systems, such as stratified water tanks integrated with heat pumps, offer a low-cost and robust solution to reduce peak grid loads through load shifting and pre-charging strategies. These systems allow heat generation to be decoupled from building heat demand while maintaining occupant thermal comfort. Despite their technical maturity, TES systems are not yet systematically integrated into building design and operation. In practice, a general and transferable framework that guides the selection, sizing, and operation of TES systems is largely missing. Developing such an integration framework can support widespread and effective implementation of TES. This could ultimately contribute to grid stability, reduced operational costs, and a more resilient and sustainable energy system. 

