\section{Thermal Energy Storage} This chapter explores the the current landscape of TES adoption in buildings in the Netherlands. TES is becoming ever more important as we transition towards a gas-free built environment but have limited electricity grid capabilities. Furthermore, this chapter identifies key obstacles that prevent universal adoption ranging from technical gaps and policy barriers. Finally, this chapter provides a brief market analysis of TES technologies currently on the market or under development.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{The Energy Transition} the Netherlands is currently undergoing a major shift in its energy strategy, aimed at completely phasing out natural gas by 2050. This transition, often referred to as being "gas-free" (\textit{van het gas af}), is a central part of the the national plan to meet international climate goals. For decades, natural gas was the standard for heating Dutch homes and buildings, but this is now being replaced by more sustainable alternatives. The focus has shifted to all-electric solutions, such as heat pumps, and the expansion of district heating networks, using the geothermal heat, to reduce $CO_2$ emissions and end reliance on local gas extraction or import.

\subsubsection*{The Decline of Natural Gas} Data from TNO, \textit{Netherlands Organisation for Applied Scientific Research}, regarding the Dutch services sector shows exactly how this transition if playing out in the real world. The graph in figure 2 highlights a steady drop in natural gas usage. By 2024, consumption fell to 92 PJ, a 4\% decrease from the year before. On average, gas use in this sector has been dropping about 4\% every year since 2014. This shows that the move away from traditional gas boilers is not just a plan, but a measurable reality. 

\subsubsection*{The Rise of Electrification} At the same time, electricity use has risen to 130 PJ. While modern appliances are becoming more efficient, the total demand for electricity is still going up. TNO attributes this primarily to the growing number of heat pumps being installed. Because heat pumps use electricity to move heat from the air or ground into a building, they are the primary reason the "green line" for electricity is rising as the "pink line" for gas falls (Dashboard Klimaatbeleid - Gebouwde Omgeving, n.d.). 

