\subsubsection{Building Energy Loads} Building energy loads can be classified into three categories based on the requirements and priorities regarding potential changing or shifting of loads:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Shiftable loads}: Represents flexible demands that can be rescheduled to align with favourable pricing periods, such as off-peak hours in real-time markets. By moving operation of appliances and electric vehicle chargers, peak demand can be mitigated without significant disruption to user comfort (Belhomme and Bouffard, 2009; Hong et al., 2015). "\textit{Within this class, it is possible to distinguish between
    load, which can be shifted but the energy profile cannot be changed, and load where the total volume must be met over a set of time periods but the profile can change within limits}" (Ottesen and Tomasgard, 2015).
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Non-shiftable loads}: These are rigid demands essential to immediate occupant needs, also known as a base-load. Such loads are often considered inflexible because their consumption patterns cannot be altered by price signals or system constraints without directly impacting the building's functionality (International Energy Agency et al., 2019). 
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Other controllable loads}: This category includes complex systems, such as HVAC units, that offer flexibility through active control strategies. Rather than simple time-shifting, these loads can be adjusted via thermostatic set-point modulation, fan speed, or dimming. This allows for the dynamic reduction of consumption during peak intervals to lower electricity use (International Energy Agency et al., 2019). 
\end{itemize}

\noindent TES serves as a transformative mechanism that converts non-shiftable load into a shiftable load. While certain demand profiles are traditionally rigid due to immediate occupant needs or fixed operational schedules, TES decouples the timing of energy generation from its eventual use. By storing energy during periods of low demand or costs, the system allows a building's total energy volume to be met flexibly across a wider time frame. 

