\subsection{Types of Thermal Energy Storage} Thermal energy storage systems can be divided into three main categories: sensible heat storage, latent heat storage, and thermochemical heat storage. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sensible heat storage:} Sensible heat storage is the simplest form of thermal energy storage. It works by increasing or decreasing the temperature of a storage medium such as water, rock, concrete, or molten salts. The material stores energy as its temperature rises and releases that energy when it cools down, without undergoing any change in physical state.
    \item \textbf{Latent heat storage:} Latent heat storage stores thermal energy by using a material’s phase change properties, such as melting or solidifying. During these phase transitions, the material absorbs or releases a significant amount of energy in the form of heat, at nearly constant temperature. Phase change materials, also known as PCMs, are commonly used in this type of storage. This method offers higher energy density than sensible heat storage, allowing large amounts of heat to be stored in relatively small volumes. PCM-enhanced installations are often more expensive than sensible TES but are a good alternative in cases with a lack of available space. 
    \item \textbf{Thermochemical heat storage:} Thermochemical heat storage uses reversible chemical reactions to store and release energy. heat input drives an endothermic reaction that separates a chemical compound into its components. In reverse, the components recombine in an exothermic process that releases the stored energy in the form of heat. This type of storage can achieve very high energy densities and allows for long-term storage with minimal losses, making it well suited for applications such as seasonal energy storage. (Thermal Energy Storage Market, 2025)
\end{itemize}
\\

