\subsection{Sensible Heat Storage} Sensible heat storage systems are already quite mature in the literature, with many systems already being deployed in buildings and districts. Listed below is an overview of the most prominent systems on the market and under development. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Buffer Storage Tank with Thermal Stratification} \textit{Status: Mature - Scale: Building }
\\

\noindent A buffer storage tank is a tank filled with water at different temperature levels. Thermal stratification refers to the natural layering of water at different temperatures inside a thermal storage tank (hot on top, warm in the middle, cold at the bottom). "\textit{Stratification allows different water temperature levels to be used directly for different purposes. The top zone of the tank is kept at the maximum temperature for instant domestic hot water production or to heating radiators, whilst at the same time the water at a lower temperature can be used for underfloor heating systems}” (Buffer Storage Tanks With Thermal Stratification, z.d.). 
\\

\noindent Depending on the heating and cooling demand of a building, the integration of buffer tank requires two tanks: A hot-water storage tank operating between 40-95 °C. Used to warm water during winter. And a chilled-water storage tank operating between 4-12 °C. Used to cool water during summer.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Aquifer Thermal Energy Storage (ATES) } \textit{Status: Mature - Scale: Building \& Campus }
\\

\noindent ATES is a seasonal thermal energy storage technology that stores heat or cold in natural underground aquifers. It is an extremely low open-loop energy system that just requires pumping of water. During winter, warm groundwater is extracted from one or more warm wells and passed through  a heat exchanger where it transfers heat to a working fluid. A heat pump then raises the temperature of the fluid, which is circulated through the building to provide heating. The cooled working fluid returns to the heat exchanger for reheating by the groundwater, and the cooled groundwater is re-injected into the aquifer via cold wells.
\\

\noindent In summer, the process is reversed. Cool groundwater is pumped from the cold wells and used to cool the working fluid through the heat exchanger, providing cooling to the building(s). This can be achieved without a heat pump in most systems (direct cooling). In some systems, however, a heat pump further reduces the working fluid's temperature. The warmed fluid is returned to the heat exchanger to be cooled by the groundwater, and the heated groundwater is re-injected into the aquifer via the warm wells for future use in winter (Jackson et al., 2024).
\\

\noindent As mentioned, ATES does require temperature upgrading before using the hot or cold water. Therefore, ATES can not be used as a direct thermal buffer. This system does store energy, but not useful heat. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Borehole Thermal Energy Storage (BTES) } \textit{Status: Mature - Scale: Building \& Campus }
\\

\noindent BTES is a type of seasonal thermal energy storage system that stores heat or cold in the ground using arrays of vertical boreholes. It is commonly used in district heating, large buildings, and renewable energy systems to balance mismatches between energy supply and demand over long periods (especially across seasons). This is a closed-loop system.
\\

\noindent The system consists of boreholes, typically arranged in a grid, containing pipes that circulate a heat-transfer fluid. Warm fluid from sources such as solar collectors, industrial processes, or excess building heat is pumped into the boreholes, storing energy in the surrounding ground. When heat is needed, the cooler fluid absorbs energy from the ground and returns to the surface, where it can be used directly or via a heat pump for space heating or hot water. This approach enables efficient seasonal storage and use of thermal energy across different heat sources (Directory, 2025).
\\

\noindent Similar to ATES, BTES does require temperature upgrading before using the hot or cold water. Therefore, BTES can not be used as a direct thermal buffer. This system does store energy, but not useful heat.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Molten Salt Tanks } \textit{Status: Mature - Scale: Utility }
\\

\noindent \textit{"The energy storage in molten salt tanks is a sensible thermal energy storage system. It is a mature technology that has been widely used in concentrating solar power (CSP) plants, for which it is essential. This technology gives the CSP plants the ability to store the energy of solar radiation from the hours of sunshine and convert it into electricity when required, adapting the energy source to the electricity demand of the grid”} (Prieto et al., 2024). 
\\

\noindent Needless to say, the scale of this TES (utility-scale system) is much bigger than building-scale. Molten tanks are not applicable to building design, since the temperatures in molten salt tanks can range from 290 - 565 °C (Torres-Madroñero et al., 2025). A heat pump in buildings for example operates at much lower temperatures: space heating: 30-55 °C and DHW: 55-70 °C, these temperatures are far below the freezing point of molten salt. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Activated Concrete Core} \textit{Status: Mature \& Under development - Scale: Building }
\\

\noindent \textit The research of Barbhuiya et al. (2023) explores the potential of, an already well-known thermal energy storage material: concrete. This paper: Thermal energy storage in concrete: A comprehensive review on fundamentals, technology and sustainability, besides covering basic characteristics of concrete, also dives deeper into optimizing/intensifying thermal energy storage in concrete.
\\

\noindent Concrete's extensive usage in construction has sparked interest in its potential as a TES medium. It boasts advantages, including availability and cost-effectiveness, making large-scale implementation viable. 
\\

\noindent\textit{“Thermal energy storage (TES) in concrete can be achieved through various methods. One common approach is sensible heat storage, where the excess thermal energy is stored by raising the temperature of the concrete itself. This can be done by circulating heated fluids through embedded pipes or by direct contact with a heat source. Another method is latent heat storage, which involves incorporating phase change materials (PCMs) into the concrete. These PCMs absorb and release thermal energy during the phase transition, effectively storing and releasing heat. Thermochemical heat storage is another technique, where chemical reactions within the concrete absorb or release heat energy for storage and retrieval. These different methods offer flexibility in designing concrete-based TES systems to meet specific energy storage requirements”} (Barbhuiya et al., 2023).

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Latent Heat Storage} PCMs have the ability to absorb and release large amounts of heat as the materials changes state from solid to liquid (melting) or from liquid to solid (freezing) at a nearly constant temperature. PCMs in building elements are very energy dense, boosting thermal mass without adding relatively much weight. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Phase Change Materials} \textit{Status: Under development - Scale: Building }
\\

\noindent \textit{“On the basis of their chemical composition, PCMs are mainly classified as organic, inorganic and eutectic materials. Each category has a range of working temperatures and thermo-physical properties; thus, they are more suitable for specific application than others”} (Al-Yasiri \& Szabó, 2020).
Paraffins and salt-hydrates are most relevant for thermal comfort applications in building design, since the temperatures (melting) of these PCMs are within a relevant range. 
\\

\noindent \textit{“Encapsulation is a suitable method to avoid the leakage issues of PCM and to enhance its compatibility with the building structure. Encapsulation is performed by covering the PCM by a shell for protection from the outside environment as well as for leakage prevention. This method is also essential to increase the heat transfer area and, hence, the thermal conductivity of PCM to ensure effective utilization of its storage capacity. The PCM can be macro-encapsulated using shells, tubes, channels and thin plates or micro-encapsulate when the micro-sized PCM is covered by unique polymeric material”} (Al-Yasiri \& Szabó, 2020).

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{PCM-enhanced Buffer Storage Tank} \textit{Status: Under development - Scale: Large building/high load profile }
\\

\noindent The research paper of Sun et al. (2025) explores the integration of PCM-materials to enhance water tanks. The paper highlights findings that conventional heat pump water heater (HPWH) loses control of its target water temperature 65\% of the time during demand-response events (periods when the heat pump is intentionally turned down or shut off to reduce grid load). 
\\

\noindent A PCM-enhanced thermal storage tank (PCM-TES) paired with an HPWH only loses control of its target temperature 11.6\% of the time under the same conditions. With PCM storage, hot water temperature inside the tanks stays stable almost all the time, even when the heat pump is not running. This is because PCM stores large amounts of energy at nearly constant temperature. It releases heat slowly and steadily, keeping water temperature within the control range. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Ice Thermal Storage} \textit{Status: Mature - Scale: Building}
\\

\noindent Ice thermal storage (ITS) is defined as a system that utilizes the latent heat of water to achieve high densities of cooling energy (freezing and melting of ice), allowing for the shifting of cooling loads to off-peak periods to reduce cost.
\\

\noindent ITS systems use the latent heat from phase transitions of water to obtain high densities of cooling energy. Water is chosen as the cold storage media because of its high latent heat fusion (334 kJ/kg). Moreover, it is low cost, environmentally-friendly, and non-toxic. ITS is used to shift high-peak demands to off-peak periods to reduce cost and minimize grid demand (Zhang et al., 2022). During night-time, ice is either formed around coils: ice-on-coil method, formed in a tank: Ice Harvester, or formed in plastic balls: Encapsulated ice storage. During day-time, the ice is used for cooling. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Thermochemical Energy Storage} Thermochemical energy storage systems constitute a less mature but highly promising alternative to sensible heat storage. These systems store energy through reversible chemical reactions, enabling higher energy storage densities and minimal storage losses over time. While their theoretical potential is well established in the literature, thermochemical storage technologies are largely still under development, with only limited demonstration at building scale. An overview of the most relevant thermochemical energy storage concepts under research and development is provided below.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Zeolite Sorption Modules} \textit{Status: Under development - Scale: Building }
\\

\noindent Thermal energy storage (TES) is an effective and economical way to balance building energy supply and demand. While sensible heat storage (SHS) and latent heat storage (LHS) systems are well-established and commercially available, thermochemical energy storage (TCES) materials offer significantly higher energy densities, approximately 3–30 times that of SHS and 2–20 times that of LHS, depending on the material. TCES also exhibits lower heat losses, supports long-term seasonal storage, and can operate at lower charging temperatures, making it particularly suitable for storing solar energy for building applications (Kant \& Pitchumani, 2022).

According to Y. N. Zhang et al. (2018), this system is suitable to supply space heating and hot water for buildings by storing solar energy or other low-grade heat. The working cycle of this system is divided into two modes, which are listed as follows. 
\\

\noindent During the charging process, the input heat is stored in the form of chemical potential by breaking the binders between the sorbate and the sorbent. Then, the sorbate and the sorbent are kept separated during the sorption period. The discharging process is motivated by the connection between the sorbent and the sorbate, which release sorption heat for heating supply when needed. Generally, the sorption materials are composed of solid adsorbent, liquid absorbent, chemical reactor, and composite sorbents. This TES system is classified as closed system and open system according to its configuration. Besides, it can also be divided into long-term storage system and short-term storage system based on the designed storage time span.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Salt Hydrate Thermochemical Storage} \textit{Status: Under development - Scale: Building }
\\

\noindent \textit{“In the charging (dehydration) step, thermal energy is used to dissociate the chemical bonds between molecules through an endothermic reaction. The dissociated materials are then kept separately (storage step). The stored thermal energy can be delivered in the form of heat, whenever required, through an exothermic reaction between the two dissociated compounds (discharging, hydration step). In this step, chemical bonds form, resulting in heat generation. After discharging, the storage material is regenerated and reused for the next cycle”} (Clark et al., 2019).
\\

\noindent A heat pump charges this TES system during off-peak periods. The salt-Hydrate thermochemical storage reactor contains salt granules (fixed or encapsulated). There is also a water vapour channel and heat exchanger for charging and discharging. In turn, a regular building heat distribution system is connected. The TES has a heat exchanger transferring heat to the building water loop. The heat losses in this system are very minimal. Heat can be stored in the chemical bonds from days to months. This is an option for seasonal storage (Balasubramanian et al., 2010). 

