\section{Methods: Proposed Framework}
\label{chap:Methods}
The proposed framework establishes a structured, eight-part methodology for the implementation of thermal energy storage (TES) systems in buildings, of which the last two parts are case specific and therefore only present in the case study section of this thesis. Nonetheless, the methods used in the last two parts inform readers of potential validation and optimisation methods. 
\\

\noindent Rather than a prescriptive guideline, this framework provides a systematic approach for navigating the integration process. The execution of each stage is intended to be adaptable, though subject to project-specific variables such as building typology, selected technology, and budgetary constraints. Chapter 4 applies this framework to a case study involving air-source heat pumps and stratified water tanks, detailing the transition from system selection to tank sizing under a defined control strategy. This application serves to validate the framework and demonstrates possible methods for optimizing the simulation model.
\\

\noindent\textbf{Framework Elements}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Thermal Demand Data}: Defining thermal demand data and identifying the specific data resolution required for the building’s thermal demand. High-resolution load profiles are necessary to ensure accuracy in sizing and overall system reliability. A transient multi-zone first-order lumped capacitance model is designed and used.
    \item \textbf{TES System per Load Curve Archetype}: Match common thermal profiles of buildings with the most compatible TES systems. Using established load curve archetypes allows for the selection of systems best suitable for specific demand signatures.
    \item \textbf{Integration into Building Design}: Incorporate the selected TES system into the building's general climate design. This stage ensures the storage infrastructure is compatible with both new and/or pre-existing HVAC installations.
    \item \textbf{Control Strategy}: Formulate the operational logic governing charging and discharging cycles. This strategy dictates the system's final required capacity by balancing occupant comfort thresholds with operational flexibility.
    \item \textbf{TES Capacity}: Determine the required storage volume and thermal capacity based on the established control Strategy to ensure peak demand is met without comfort violations.
    \item \textbf{Simulation Model}: Capture the all preceding steps into a Python-based simulation model.
    \item \textbf{Validation - Case Study}: Verify the reliability of the integrated TES system, and customize the simulation model based on the case study. 
    \item \textbf{Optimisation - Case Study}: Optimise the variables inside the simulation model to minimize TES required capacity and maximize efficiency.
\end{enumerate}
