\subsubsection{Thermal Demand Calculation} The thermal demand of a building is typically calculated through a dynamic energy balance. A robust energy balance framework is provided by the NEN-EN-ISO 52016-1. This approach calculates the energy required to maintain a stable indoor temperature by balancing the heat losses against the internal and external heat gains for every hour of the year. Performing this balancing calculation for 8760 hours provides a comprehensive profile of a building's annual thermal demand. The following equation illustrates the most simple version of this energy balance:
\\

\begin{equation}
    Q(t) = Q_{loss}(t) - Q_{gain}(t)
\end{equation}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent To better understand the key drivers of the building’s thermal demand, we can break the energy balance down into its primary components. This equation accounts for the building's physical properties, environmental factors, and the buffering effect of its thermal mass:
\\

\begin{equation}
Q(t) = [ H \cdot (\theta_{int} - \theta_{ext}(t)) ] - [ Q_{int}(t) + Q_{sol}(t) ] - Q_{stor}(t)
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $Q(t)$ is the thermal demand at time-step (e.g., hour) $t$ [kWh].
\item $H$ is the total heat loss coefficient [kW/K].
\item $\theta_{int}$ is the internal set-point temperature [${}^\circ$C].
\item $\theta_{ext}(t)$ is the ambient outdoor temperature at hour $t$ [${}^\circ$C].
\item $Q_{int}(t)$ represents internal heat gains [kWh].
\item $Q_{sol}(t)$ represents solar heat gains [kWh].
\item $Q_{stor}(t)$ is the thermal energy stored or released by the building's thermal mass [kWh].
\end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent The rest of this thesis will refer to the thermal demand per climatization zone per time-step as $Q_{zone}(t)$. 

