\subsubsection{Identifying Climatization Zones} A climatization zone is defined as a specific space or grouping of thermal spaces within a building that share uniform heating, cooling, and ventilation requirements. These areas are grouped based on shared temperature set-points, emitter system, occupancy patterns, internal heat gains, and external environmental exposures, such as solar orientation and wind. Strategic zoning allows for the rationalization of varying thermal demands ($Q_{zone}$) across a building's footprint (Thermal Zones in Building Design, n.d.).
\\

\noindent For the purposes of this study, references to thermal demand per zone ($Q_{zone}$) specifically refer to the aggregate demand of a defined climatization zone. The group of thermal zones within a specific climatization zone will be governed by the same control strategy, discussed in part 4 of the general framework.
\\

