\subsubsection{Obtaining Thermal Demand Dataset} To maintain a generalized framework, this study offers two distinct pathways for acquiring thermal demand profiles. The first treats the demand as given input data, utilizing thermal datasets exported from external third-party software. The second provides an integrated modelling option:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{External simulation software}: External building performance simulation (BPS) software, such as Vabi Elements which is an industry standard in the Dutch construction and engineering industry, can provide high-fidelity thermal demand datasets. This software, along with other BPS software provides a specialized environment for calculating hourly thermal loads, energy labels, and indoor climate performance. 
    \item \textbf{Simplified energy balance model}: Since external simulation software is not always available as an option to generate thermal demand datasets from buildings and to maintain an applicable general framework, this thesis provides an alternative route by modelling a multi-zone, first-order lumped capacitance model based on the hourly energy balance method of the ISO 13790 / NEN-EN-ISO 52016-1. Unlike the fixed external datasets, this internal model allows for the direct manipulation of parameters to influence the resulting thermal data, serving as a flexible, thermal demand dataset calculator within this study. This model can be found in the \textit{Appendix D}. 
\end{enumerate}

