    \vspace{2pt}
    {\footnotesize \textit{Adapted from Almutairi et al.(2015).}}
\end{figure}

\noindent The Load Duration Curve is a statistical tool rather than a chronological schedule. Because it sorts annual demand from highest to lowest, it shows the frequency of a load but removes the context of when it occurred. Consequently, two buildings with entirely different usage patterns, an office peaking at noon and a residence peaking at night, could share an identical curve if their total energy volume and peak magnitudes match.

While this makes the curve unsuitable for daily operational control, a LDC allows for the immediate identification of the maximum power capacity and total energy volume (area under the curve) required to size the physical components of a Thermal Energy Storage system.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Zonal Chronological Hourly Load} While the Load Duration Curve provides a statistical overview of annual demand, it obscures the operational timing necessary for sizing a TES system. To accurately determine the required power capacity and discharge duration, this research utilizes the Chronological Hourly Load (CHL). Unlike the LDC, the CHL preserves the temporal sequence of demand, allowing for a realistic assessment of how a thermal profile fluctuates over time. The CHL is analysed across three distinct temporal scales, with each revealing different requirements for the storage medium:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Daily scale}: At a daily scale, the chronological load identifies the intra-day variance and peak-power intensity. This reveals the necessary "thermal responsiveness" of the TES. A thermal profile characterized by high-magnitude, short-duration fluctuations requires a system with high heat-transfer rates to ensure that the discharge power can meet rapid transitions in the demand-side requirements. 
    \item \textbf{Weekly scale}: Extending the analysis to a weekly time-frame uncovers periodic occupancy cycles and operational idle periods, such as weekends. This scale is critical for assessing storage requirements and minimizing energy losses during periods of inactivity-e.g., by determining the necessary level of insulation for a water tank to prevent stored thermal energy from dissipating over an idle weekend.
    \item \textbf{Annual scale}: Viewing hourly data over a full year maps the seasonal phase shift between energy generation and peak thermal load. This long-term chronological sequence is the fundamental metric for calculating the required energy-to-power ratio. It determines the transition from short-term buffer solutions to high-capacity seasonal TES, which must bridge multi-month gaps between energy surplus and deficit. ATES and BTES are examples of systems characterized by a high energy-to-power ratio, designed to manage substantial volumes of energy over extended durations.
\end{itemize}
