\subsubsection{Grid Congestion Time-frame} Grid congestion adds another layer of necessity to the temporal analysis of thermal demand. While traditional TES selection is based on the building's needs, grid-aware TES selection is based on the electrical grid's capacity to supply that energy. This shift moves the focus primarily to the daily and weekly timescales, where grid congestion occurs. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Daily scale}: On a daily basis, grid congestion typically occurs during "peak" hours when demand spikes just as solar generation drops. In the Netherlands, morning peaks (7:00-09:00) are often described as "Wake-up" spikes. Residential demand for heat and hot water coincides with office buildings powering up and the start of industrial processes. The evening peak (17:00-21:00) is the most critical window, when the grid is congested the most. By analysing the chronological load, it is possible to identify these congested windows and determine the thermal demand in that period. 
    \item \textbf{Weekly scale}: A building’s chronological hourly load over a typical week provides a representative demand profile, as it captures the interplay between daily peak power requirements and weekly occupancy rhythms, such as weekend setbacks. This scale is crucial for managing grid congestion, as it identifies the capacity needed to shift loads away from daily "rush hours" while ensuring the system can bridge multi-day periods of high grid stress or low renewable availability.
    \item \textbf{Annual scale}: While daily and weekly scales define operational strategy, the annual scale is critical for identifying the "design day". This is the most extreme period of peak demand and minimum energy availability in a year. By capturing these annual extremes, the TES system can be sized to ensure it remains reliable during the worst-case scenarios, preventing a total depletion of thermal reserves when the building's demand is at its highest and the grid is most congested. 
\end{itemize}
\\

