\subsubsection{Load Curve Archetypes and Suitable TES system allocation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Base-load dominated} (The Flatline): This curve represents facilities with constant thermal requirements, such as hospitals or data centres. The profile is characterized by minimal fluctuation, where thermal load remains consistently high throughout the day and night without significant peaks or valleys. 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{Suitable TES:} \textcolor{myblue}{Stratified Water Tanks | TABS | Phase Change Materials (PCM)}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Bimodal} (The M-curve): Typical of residential buildings, this curve shows two distinct peaks in the morning and evening, separated by a midday valley. This archetype requires a TES capable of daily cycling: charging during the low-demand midday period to discharge during the high-demand peaks.
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{Suitable TES:} \textcolor{myblue}{Stratified Water Tanks | PCM-enhanced Storage | High-Performance Sensible Tanks}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Diurnal \& Extended peak} (The Plateau): Most common in commercial offices and retail, this profile features a sustained high demand that lasts for the hours of a regular workday. The TES selection for this archetype must prioritize energy capacity to ensure the system does not deplete before the occupancy period ends. 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{Suitable TES:} \textcolor{myblue}{Ice Thermal Storage | Stratified Water Tanks | PCM | TABS}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Fast-response} (The Needle Peak): This curve is defined by sudden, intense spikes in demand over short durations. This curve is typical for event-driven spaces like theatres. The focus for this archetype is high power delivery during event periods. Recharging of the thermal buffer is done during low demand periods. 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{Suitable TES:} \textcolor{myblue}{Stratified Water Tanks | High-conductivity PCM | Short-term Buffer Storage}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Seasonal} (U-Shape): This profile tracks long-term shifts in demand across months rather than hours, reflecting the transition from winter heating to summer cooling. This archetype requires large-scale storage solutions that can bridge the gap between energy availability in one season and peak demand in another. 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{Suitable TES:} \textcolor{myblue}{ATES | BTES | Zeolite Sorption | Salt-hydrate Storage}
        \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
\\

