\subsubsection{Centralized TES} In a centralized TES setup, the system acts as a single "thermal vault" that stores the building's energy reserve in one location. By storing thermal energy in a large source tank or thermal battery, the design lowers the cost per unit of energy stored and makes maintenance much simpler (Centralized Vs Distributed Energy Storage Systems: Pros And Cons, 2025). Because the surface area of a single thermal buffer is often less compared to multiple small micro-buffers, the standing heat losses are lower. However, this assumption is only true if the micro-buffers are the same TES medium: PCM buffers have a higher heat density and require less volume compared to a water tank buffer, when storing the same amount of energy. 
\\

\noindent A centralized TES layout is ideal for load levelling the primary heat source, allowing a boiler or heat pump to run at a steady, high-efficiency rate while the storage handles the building's fluctuating peaks. Since the energy is stored in a central location, a well-designed hydraulic system is needed. 
