\subsubsection{Hybrid of Centralized TES \& Decentralized TES} The hybrid configuration integrates a central thermal buffer with decentralized micro-buffers. By utilizing a central reservoir as a hydraulic separator, the heat source can maintain a consistent, lower baseline temperature to satisfy general building demands, thereby improving overall system efficiency. The local TES units provide supplemental storage capacity for specific high-demand zones, allowing these zones to be charged independently during off-peak periods—often utilizing materials with higher energy density than the central buffer. This enables the local units to provide the high-intensity energy required for peak shifting or specific high-demand climatization zones without requiring the central system to operate at higher temperature set-points or be sized much larger to be able to deliver this high energy demand. 
\\

\noindent However, this setup requires sophisticated control logic to synchronize the charging of the central thermal buffer and the local micro-buffers without hydraulic interference, which increases the complexity of the operational strategy
