\subsubsection{Compatibility of Operating Temperatures} All topologies have to take into consideration that different TES systems as well as standard emitters operate with specific temperatures. Generally it is favourable when the operating temperatures of the systems are within a small temperature range. In an ideal scenario the operating temperatures for all systems is the same. This would be the most efficient and exergy preserving scenario. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Central TES}: In case of uniform temperatures among the entire system, $T_{storage} = T_{emitters}$. If the operating temperatures differ among the emitters, the highest required temperature dictates the maximum temperature output of the TES, resulting in $T_{storage} > T_{emitters}$ for lower temperature emitters. This might result in significant thermal efficiency degradation of heat sources due to, in most cases, unnecessary temperature lift. Thermal stratification in sensible heat storage tanks is a way of combatting this by preserving exergy. Nonetheless, this is not a perfect solution.
    \item \textbf{De-central TES}: In case of a fully decentralized TES configuration with uniform temperatures, the heat source can charge the TES (such as PCM-modules) with a steady temperature and treat the building as a giant thermal battery. In case of varying operating temperatures for both fully decentralized TES and partial decentralized TES configurations, the system would have to be able to supply those different temperatures using specialized valves, stratification, temperature mixing strategies or other solutions. 
    \item \textbf{Hybrid}: In case of uniform temperatures, the system would be redundant. There is little benefit to having two layers of storage if the temperatures are the same, unless there is little available space in the central storage hub. In case of varying temperatures, the heat source charges the central thermal buffer at a lower temperature for baseline heating. For charging the local thermal buffer units (such as PCM-modules) that might require higher charging temperatures, with a bypass of the central tank, the heat source could directly feed the local units with a temporary higher temperature output. The local TES units provide the extra temperature lift. Disadvantages include complex piping and control systems.
\end{itemize}

