\subsubsection{Integration with Renewable Energy} On-site renewable energy generation mitigates grid congestion by decentralizing the electricity supply, allowing a building to satisfy a significant portion of its thermal and electrical demand internally. The added renewable energy can be used to generate and store heat for a building, essentially increasing the building's total available electricity supply throughout the day. Especially during grid-congested times, this added electricity or heat is very convenient. 
\\

\noindent Renewable energy sources, PV (photovoltaic) and PVT (photovoltaic-thermal) panels in particular can be well-integrated into a thermal buffer system. PVT panels combine two technologies: solar PV and solar thermal (hot water). Both heat and energy are generated from solar irradiation (Useesolar, n.d.). Over the years these panels have become increasingly popular for buildings. Depending on the type of thermal energy storage, either PV or PVT is a more compatible option for a certain system setup. This chapter will cover the integration of solar collectors with TES systems, however, the possible system configurations are not limited to the examples discussed. 
