\subsubsection*{Thermochemical Heat Storage} According to the review "Applications of low-temperature thermochemical energy storage systems" by Lin et al. (2020), TCES systems can be categorized as open or closed based on operational mode, both systems most compatible with PVT integration for low-temperature sorbents (e.g., Salt Hydrates). High-temperature sorbents (e.g., Zeolites) require an extra temperature lift from a heat source, since the PVT cannot provide these required temperatures. 
\\
    
\noindent Closed systems recycle the reactant internally. During storage, vapour is condensed into a liquid and kept in a tank; it is later evaporated to trigger the discharge reaction. While these systems are complex and expensive due to vacuum requirements, they allow for seasonal heating and cooling using a wide range of chemicals. Open systems exchange vapour directly with the surrounding air. These systems are simpler, cheaper, and offer high energy density because they do not need internal gas storage. However, these systems are limited to water-based reactions and require fans and humidifiers to operate, which can affect indoor humidity.
