\subsection{Part 4: Control Strategy} This part of the framework defines the operational logic required to manage the interaction between the building's thermal demand, the thermal system, and the selected storage mediums. While the previous step established the physical integration and system topology, this part focuses on the dynamic governance of energy flows. By conceptualizing the system components as discrete functional nodes, a hierarchical priority is established to satisfy specific operational goals.
\\

\noindent The following sections evaluate both active and passive control strategies, ranging from specific TES operating modes to the utilization of the building’s inherent thermal mass. Central to this analysis is the "switching strategy," which determines the optimal dispatch order based on the available recovery window (time available to recharge the TES) and the characteristic saturation time of each medium. This chapter provides the control strategy necessary to transition from a static building design to a responsive thermal management system. The final capacity of the TES system, determined in part 6 of the framework, is a direct result of the control strategy.
\\

