\subsubsection{System Priority Order} To establish a versatile control strategy, this study treats thermal demand, the heat source (the thermal system), the TES system, and thermal mass as discrete functional nodes. However, more nodes can be added if wanted. These nodes serve as the logical basis for defining the system's operational priority, allowing for a tailored energy hierarchy per building that meets specific objectives such as grid-congestion mitigation or cost optimization. 
\\

\noindent This hierarchical approach allows the energy flow to be tailored to a building by determining which node takes precedence during charging or discharging cycles. The priority order of the nodes are based on type of load profile, system preferences, boundary conditions, or a pre-determined control strategy/climate design. While the fundamental nodal framework remains consistent across different scenarios, the specific sequence of interaction (of the nodes) defines the system's behaviour. Once the main nodal order is established, detailed sub-control layers are implemented to manage the precise operational logic within each individual component.
\\

\noindent \textit{Figure 26} shows examples of nodal sequences. To provide more contextualization, the following examples compare strategy A and B:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Strategy A}: This configuration utilizes a fixed priority sequence starting with, what is always, the thermal demand. During grid congestion, the heat source is deactivated, forcing the system to rely on storage. The thermal mass is dispatched first to provide a high-inertia buffer, while the TES system remains in reserve, activating only after the structural mass is depleted. This maximizes passive energy reduction before utilizing the dedicated buffer.

    \item \textbf{Strategy B}: In this approach, the heat source remains active but is modulated during grid congestion. To maintain high temperature set-point precision, the TES system is prioritized as the primary secondary source to assist the generation equipment. The thermal mass serves as the last buffer only utilised after the active storage capacity (TES) is exhausted. This strategy prioritizes immediate thermal stability over passive efficiency.
\end{itemize}
