\subsubsection{Active Strategy: Operating Modes} Storage performance is inherently tied to its control logic. ASHRAE (2012, p. 822) notes that the specifics of equipment operation, control valve functions, and set-points are all determined by the chosen operation mode. This directly impacts the required energy capacity ($E_{\text{TEScapacity}}$) and the resulting TES volume ($V_{final}$). A combination of operating modes is also possible. In that case, a hierarchy of operating modes needs to be established. This hierarchy will be the control logic that the system follows. Prime examples of operation modes are, but not limited to:

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Charging with no load}: This mode is generally characterized by a straightforward control sequence where the (heat or cold) generation equipment, such as heat pumps, operates at maximum capacity with constant supply temperature set-points. Charging continuous at a constant flow rate until the storage is fully charged or the available period ends. The scenario assumes that all designated equipment is dedicated solely to storage purposes. 
    \item \textbf{Charging while meeting load}: Necessary for buildings with continuous thermal demands, this mode is more complex. The control logic operates the heat or cold generation equipment at maximum capacity, but the load is prioritized. Only the capacity not required to meet the instantaneous  demand is diverted to the TES. This necessitates careful consideration of system piping (series or parallel) to ensure both the load and the storage receive adequate thermal energy.
    \item \textbf{Meeting load from discharge only}: Often referred to as a full storage or load shifting, this mode involves deactivating the primary heat or cold generating equipment entirely (decoupling). The entire thermal load is met by the storage system. This strategy is the primary driver for determining the required energy capacity ($V_S$) for a specific discharge window, as it defines the duration the TES must operate as the sole provider. 
    \item \textbf{Meeting load from discharge and direct equipment}: Utilized in partial storage strategies, this mode requires complex control sequences to regulate the proportion of the load met by the TES versus the primary equipment. These sequences must dynamically select equipment operation and capacity. This interaction is best evaluated using a continuous energy balance ($V_C$) to identify the largest cumulative deficit during peak or constrained periods. 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{\textit{Generation priority control}}: The primary heat or cooling source is operated at a constant or optimized capacity, often based on its peak efficiency point. The TES is utilized only to "shave" the remaining peak demand that exceeds the equipment's limit. This minimizes storage cycling but requires larger generation plant capacity.
        \item \textbf{\textit{Storage priority control}}: The control sequence prioritizes the discharge of the TES to meet as much of the demand as possible. The primary generation equipment remains inactive or operates at a minimum until the storage discharge rate or temperature can no longer satisfy the load. The maximizes the utilization of the stored energy, but carries a higher risk of premature storage depletion.
        \item \textbf{\textit{Constant proportion control}}: This strategy modulates both the storage discharge and the primary equipment simultaneously to meet the load in a fixed or variable ratio. By dynamically adjusting the contribution to each source, the system ensures that the storage capacity is distributed evenly across the entire discharge window. 
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

