\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Reduction of transmission heat loss}: According to Fourier's law, the rate of heat loss ($Q = U \cdot A \cdot \Delta T$) is directly proportional to the temperature difference between the interior and exterior. By allowing the building temperature to drift within the defined comfort temperature swing during peak periods, the average $\Delta T$ is reduced. Thereby lowering the total energy lost to the environment compared to maintaining a constant set-point (using the buffer tank first).
    \item \textbf{Exergy and reserve management}: Structural mass provides high thermal inertia but low discharge flexibility. By utilizing this slow energy source first, the system preserves the high-grade thermal energy within the TES, especially sensible TES,  as a high-power reserve. This ensures that the TES remains available to provide a rapid thermal response. Besides supplying thermal energy to stay within the swing temperature limit, saving the buffer for last can serve against unforeseen spikes or extended occupancy.
\end{itemize}
\\

