\subsubsection{Thermal Mass Calculation}
\label{Thermal mass calculation}

\subsubsection*{Thermal Mass Constant} To quantify the building's inherent energy storage potential, this study employs a methodology based on the specific thermal capacity standards for \textit{Dutch energy performance}. The thermal mass of each zone is estimated by correlating its structural characteristics, in particular the material density of floors and walls and ceilings, with its total floor area. From the provided performance data, the energy required to raise the temperature (or vice versa: the energy released when lowering the temperature) of the internal structure per square meter of floor area can be estimated. 
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{Thermal mass Cspec table.pdf}
    
    % This command forces the caption to be small only for THIS figure
    \captionsetup{font=footnotesize} 
    
    \caption{Dutch Energy Performance table}
    \label{fig:stratification_comparison}

    \vspace{2pt}
    {\footnotesize \textit{Adapted from Energy performance of buildings – Determination method (NTA 8800:2024), by Netherlands Standardization Institute, 2024.}}
\end{figure} 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent The specific capacity is converted into a functional thermal mass constant ($M_{zone}$) expressed in [kWh/K]. This constant allows the TES sizing model to treat the structure as a secondary thermal buffer: absorbing energy during charging, releasing energy during discharge. The conversion is defined as:
\\

\begin{equation}
    M_{zone} = \frac{Area_{floor} \cdot C_{spec} \cdot 1000}{3.6 \times 10^6}
\end{equation}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent While utilizing the Dutch Energy Performance table allows for a fast standardized calculation of zonal heat capacity, it introduces some drawbacks:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Instantaneous heat exchange}: The model assumes "lumped capacitance", where the entire structural mass reacts to the temperature changes immediately. In reality materials have thermal lag, meaning it takes time to charge or discharge the heat. Concrete for example, depending on the total mass could have a thermal lag of hours. A more realistic estimation of the thermal mass capacity would be to introduce an "Effective Thermal Mass" factor, which would result in a portion of the theoretical mass.  
    \item \textbf{Surface area versus volume}: The estimation relies solely on floor area. It fails to distinguish between a thick concrete slab and multiple thin partition walls, which have different heat transfer rates despite having the same total mass. 
\end{itemize}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection*{Zone Share of Heat Source Output} For the (re)charging of the thermal mass of each zone, a second control layer regarding the thermal hunger per zone is present: 
\\

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sequential recharging of zones}: This strategy allows the zones to be recharged sequentially. If it is adamant that temperatures, and therefore thermal mass, of specific zones are restored to the set-point temperature, a sequence of recharging can be implemented. The high-priority zones would be recharged before lower-priority zones.
\\

\item \textbf{Proportional recharging of zones}: The proportional division would be based on the "thermal hunger" in [kWh] of each zone. For each zone $i$:
\end{itemize}
\\

\begin{equation}
        Hunger_i = (T_{max,i} - T_{current,i}) \times M_{zone, i}
\end{equation}
\\

    Where:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $M_{zone, i}$ is thermal mass constant expressed in [kWh/K] of zone $i$.
        \item $T_{max,i}$ is the set-point temperature of zone $i$.
        \item $T_{current,i}$ is the current temperature of zone $i$.
    \end{itemize}
    \bigskip

\noindent The available surplus power from the heat source is then distributed using a ratio:
\\

\begin{equation}
    ZoneShare_i = \left( \frac{Hunger_i}{\sum Hunger} \right) \cdot Q_{surplus}
\end{equation}
    \\

    Where:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $ZoneShare_i$: Power allocated to zone $i$ [kW].
        \item $Hunger_i$: The energy deficit of zone $i$, defined as the total thermal energy required to raise the zone temperature to its respective set-point temperature ($T_{set}$) [kWh].
        \item $\sum Hunger$: The cumulative energy deficit of all zones in the building [kWh].
        \item $Q_{surplus}$: The available thermal power from the heat source remaining after satisfying the total instantaneous building demand [kW].
    \end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent Larger zones require a greater quantity of thermal energy to achieve a 1K temperature rise due to their higher thermal mass. The logic compensates by allocating a higher share of the available thermal power to these zones. Zones with a larger temperature drop have a bigger energy deficit. The logic directs a larger fraction of the thermal power to these areas, prioritizing the zones furthest from their comfort set-points. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Recovery Window: Switching Strategy} Regarding recharging strategy, the selection between structural thermal mass and dedicated TES system is primarily dictated by the available recovery window relative to the characteristic response time of the storage medium. Because heat transfer into the building structure involves significant thermal lag, the structural mass acts as a high-inertia storage asset that requires extended charging durations to achieve meaningful thermal saturation. 
\\

\noindent Consequently, during extended recovery periods (in between net congested hours), the control strategy prioritizes structural pre-charging to stabilize the Mean Radiant Temperature (MRT) and provide a high-capacity, passive baseload buffer. Conversely, during short windows in between net congestion, the logic shifts to the dedicated TES: its higher power density and optimized heat exchange surfaces, allow it to reach a full State of Charge (SoC) rapidly. This provides a high-grade, low-inertia energy reserve that can be dispatched with minimal delay to meet instantaneous demand when the primary thermal energy generation source is constrained. Switching charging strategy based on the recharge window is necessary when the thermal mass cannot be recharged fast enough.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection*{Temporal Feasibility Check} In the context of thermal management, $t_{sat}$ (Saturation Time) represents the temporal requirement for a specific storage medium to reach its maximum energy capacity or defined temperature set-point under current power constraints. It serves as the speed limit of the storage strategy, accounting for both the available power of the heat source and the physical resistance of the building materials. The relationship between $t_{sat}$ and the recovery window ($\Delta t_{window}$) is the primary driver for system efficiency and feasibility:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If the recovery window is shorter than $t_{sat}$, the structural mass cannot be meaningfully charged to feel a difference in temperature, making the high-inertia storage an ineffective target for energy. 
    \item By comparing these two values, the control logic can pivot to the medium that provides the best fit: prioritizing the TES system (for example a fast charged sensible buffer tank) when time is scarce and the structural mass when the window is long enough to overcome thermal lag. 
\end{itemize}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent The actual durations [hours] required for each zone to absorb the energy, incorporating a utilization factor ($\eta$) to account for the thermal lag and surface resistance of the mass: 
\\

\begin{equation}
t_{sat, struct_i} = \frac{Hunger_i}{ZoneShare_i \cdot \eta}
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\eta$: The Utilization Factor (e.g., 1.2). It represents the effectiveness of the heat transfer path into the mass.
\end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent The system-wide saturation time ($t_{sat, struct}$) is calculated as the duration required for the available surplus thermal power to satisfy the collective energy deficit of all zones:
\\

\begin{equation}
    t_{sat, struct} = \frac{\sum Hunger_{i}}{Q_{surplus} \cdot \eta}
\end{equation}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent The fundamental logic that determines the priority of the charging sequence:
\\

$$
\text{Strategy} = 
\begin{cases} 
\text{Prioritize Structural Mass} & \text{if } \Delta t_{window} \geq t_{sat, struct} \\
\text{Prioritize Dedicated TES} & \text{if } \Delta t_{window} < t_{sat, struct}
\end{cases}
$$
\medskip

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent The saturation time for the dedicated Thermal Energy Storage tank ($t_{sat, tes}$) is calculated based on the energy required to reach the maximum capacity of the TES:
\\

\begin{equation}
    t_{sat, tes} = \frac{E_{tank, gap}}{Q_{surplus}}
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $E_{TES, gap}$ is the remaining energy capacity in the TES (kWh), calculated as $E_{TES, max} - E_{TES, current}$.
\end{itemize}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

