\subsection{Part 5: TES Sizing Method} While the previous step established the operational priority of the nodes for the control strategy, Step 6 determines the energy storage capacity required to support that logic while maintaining occupant thermal comfort. This stage utilizes the time-series data from part 1 and the control strategy from part 4 of the framework to calculate the necessary Thermal Energy Storage (TES) capacity and the resulting physical dimensions of the storage system. The sizing process evaluates three distinct volumetric requirements: demand-based capacity ($V_S$), continuous energy balancing ($V_C$), and hydraulic stability volume ($V_H$).
\\

\begin{figure}[H] % [H] forces it to stay exactly HERE
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{Workflow stage 5 pdf.pdf} 
    \captionsetup{font=footnotesize}
    \caption{\label{fig:frog}Part 5 of the framework}
    \label{fig:workflow}
\end{figure}
\\

\noindent Determining the correct storage size requires distinguishing between static and dynamic assessments. The static assessment, $V_S$, functions as a design snapshot. It is calculated based on a single, isolated event, such as a fixed 4-hour grid-constrained window, to establish a baseline capacity for early-stage planning. Because it focuses on one event, it does not account for the influence of preceding days or the variability of an entire year.
\\

\noindent In contrast, the dynamic assessment, $V_C$, evaluates the system performance over an entire operational time-series. Unlike $V_S$, this approach is highly dependent on the system’s state from previous days, ensuring the storage remains functional across consecutive periods where fluctuating energy supply might prevent a full recharge.
\\

\noindent Finally, to ensure the system can physically move the stored energy, we calculate $V_H$. This checks that the storage volume is sufficient to handle the instantaneous power and flow constraints of the hydraulic circuit, bridging the gap between stored energy capacity and real-time delivery requirements. In this case $V_H$ is only relevant for hydraulic systems. It functions as an example for checking if the capacity volume is also compatible with specific system medium and heat source constraints.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Defining the Discharge Window}

\textit{Step 2: Thermal demand} of the workflow described the need for the thermal demand per climatization zone, provided as a time-series dataset. The required span of this dataset (yearly, weekly, daily, or else) depends on the chosen design period that the TES is sized for. This is generally the worst-case scenario with the highest loads,  or a typical, most occurring, time-frame.
\\

\noindent A critical next step in TES sizing is determining the buffer period. This is the specific duration in which the TES is expected to be the primary heat provider, allowing to decouple the electric heat source. This window is typically defined by the operational strategy, such as shifting the load away from expensive peak-tariff hours or bridging the gap during periods when primary heat sources (such as heat pumps or solar collectors) are inactive due to grid congestion constraints. In case the primary heat source ($\dot{Q}_{supply}$) is modulated down instead of being completely inactive, $E_{\text{TEScapacity}}$ should account for: $\dot{Q}_{demand} - \dot{Q}_{supply}$ per zone or as a cumulative: 
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{equation}
E_{\text{TEScapacity}} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( |Q_{\text{zone},i}| \cdot t_i \right)
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $E_{\text{TEScapacity}}$ is the total energy demand for a given discharge window [kWh].
    \item $Q_{\text{zone},i}$ is the absolute thermal demand (both heating and cooling) of zone $i$ [kW].
    \item $t_i$ is the specific discharge window duration for zone $i$ [hour(s)].
\end{itemize}
\\

\begin{equation}
E_{\text{TEScapacity}} = S \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( |Q_{\text{zone},i}| \cdot t_i \right)
\end{equation}
\\

Where, in case of sensible TES (a tank in particular):
\begin{itemize}
    \item {$S = 1.1$ (10\%):} Use this if the tank and pipes are extremely well-insulated and located inside the thermal envelope of the building.
    \item {$S = 1.2$ (20\%):} The standard ``safe'' engineering margin for typical indoor installations.
    \item {$S = 1.25+$ (25\%+):} Use this if the tank is located outside or in an unheated plant room to account for higher standing losses.
\end{itemize}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Volumetric Formulas} The final TES volume $V_{final}$ is determined using the selection function defined in Equation 1. This function selects the governing volume requirement from three independent design criteria, ensuring the system satisfies all operational constraints.
\\

\begin{equation}
    V_{final} = \max \left( V_S, V_H, V_C \right)
\end{equation}
\\

or
\\

\begin{equation}
    V_{final} = \max(V_S, V_C) + V_H
\end{equation}
\\

\noindent Both formula $V_S$ and $V_H$ are quasi-static: demand input data is derived from a dynamic model. However, calculating for a certain design day assumes constant values for $\Delta T$, resulting in a more static calculation. $V_S$ calculates the total capacity needed for a certain discharge window. $V_H$ calculates the volume needed to account for instantaneous power \& flow.
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{Formulas V.pdf}
    
    % This command forces the caption to be small only for THIS figure
    \captionsetup{font=footnotesize} 
    
    \caption{Final volume formula options depending on context, available data, complexity, and TES type.}
    
