\subsubsection*{1. Volume based on Static Demand - $V_S$} Formula $V_S$ calculates the volume ($V_S$) required to store the total thermal energy needed for your discharge window ($t$):
\\

\begin{equation}
V_S = \frac{E_{\text{TEScapacity}} \cdot 3600 \cdot S}{\rho \cdot \Delta u \cdot \eta_{str}}
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $V_S$ is the required storage volume based on energy [m$^3$ or L].
    \item $E_{\text{TEScapacity}}$ is the total energy demand [kWh].
    \item $S$ is the safety factor (e.g.,, 1.1 -- 1.25).
    \item $\rho$ is the density of the storage medium [kg/m$^3$].
    \item $\Delta u$ is the specific energy density of the medium [kJ/kg].
    \item $\eta_{str}$ is the thermal storage efficiency (typically 0.85 -- 0.95).
\end{itemize}
\bigskip

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent The specific energy density for the following types on thermal energy storage:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Sensible Storage
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\Delta u = c_p \cdot \Delta T_{avg}$
        \item $\Delta T_{avg} = \frac{\sum (Q_i \cdot \Delta T_i)}{Q_{total}}$
        \item $c_p$: Specific heat capacity of the medium.
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item Latent Storage (PCM)
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\Delta u = (c_{p,s} \cdot \Delta T_s) + L + (c_{p,l} \cdot \Delta T_l)$
        \item $\Delta T_s, \Delta T_l$: Temperature ranges of solid and liquid phases.
        \item $c_{p,s}, c_{p,l}$: Specific heat capacities of solid and liquid phases.
        \item $L$: Latent heat of fusion.
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item Thermochemical Storage (TCS)
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\Delta u = \Delta H_r$
        \item $\Delta H_r$: Enthalpy of reaction or adsorption.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}
\medskip

\subsubsection*{2. Volume based on Continuous Energy Balance - $V_C$} While $V_S$ focusses on a discrete event, $V_C$ evaluates the system's performance over a continuous time-series (e.g., 1 year - 8760 hours) for a specific selected control strategy. This approach accounts for the zone temperatures and the State of Charge (SoC) of the TES by identifying the largest cumulative deficit between demand and supply ensuring the storage does not deplete during extended periods of low supply. 
\\

\noindent The hourly demand (${Q}_{demand,t}$) used in $V_C$ is defined as the aggregated zonal demand ($Q_{zonal}$) to ensure the TES capacity accounts for simultaneous heating and cooling requirements across all $n$ zones. ${Q}_{demand,i}$ is the energy demand resulting from the control strategy. In case thermal mass is utilised first to let the zone temperatures drift, ${Q}_{demand,t}$ is the energy demand required to maintain the indoor temperature of the zones within the maximum allowed $T_{swing}$.
\\

\noindent In case of a dedicated TES for cooling and a separate TES for heating, calculating the volume for both TES requires two versions of the $V_C$ formula, one for a heating demand and one for a cooling demand.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Determine the building-level net energy flow [kW] for every hour ($t$): 
    \\

    \begin{equation}
        Q_{demand, t} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_{zone, i, t}
    \end{equation}
    \\

    \begin{equation}
        Q_{net, t} = Q_{demand, t} - Q_{supply, t}
    \end{equation}
    \\

\noindent In case of grid congestion capping the thermal supply ($Grid\_Limit_t$ converted from [kVA] to [kW]: 
    \\
    
    \begin{equation}
        Q_{net, t} = Q_{demand, t} - \min(Q_{source, t}, \text{Grid\_Limit}_t)
    \end{equation}
    \\
    
    \item To find the cumulative energy deficit for the building at any time ($t$) in [kWh], the net energy flow for the current time-step ($Q_{net, t} \cdot \Delta t$) is added to the deficit accumulated in the previous time-step ($E_{cum, t-1}$). To account for the "fully charged" buffer constraint, where the tank is full and cannot store additional surplus energy, $E_{cum, t}$ is reset to 0 whenever a surplus occurs that exceeds the existing debt:
    \\

    \begin{equation}
        E_{cum, t} = \max \left( 0, E_{cum, t-1} + (Q_{net, t} \cdot \Delta t) \right)
    \end{equation}
    \\
    
    \item Find the maximum energy gap in the time-series (e.g., for a year $t$ = 8760 hours): 
    \\
    
    \begin{equation}
        E_{max\_gap} = \max(E_{cum, 1}, E_{cum, 2}, \dots, E_{cum, n})
    \end{equation}
    \\
    
    \item Use the maximum energy debt in the volumetric volume formula: 
    \\
    
    \begin{equation}
        V_C = \frac{E_{max\_gap} \cdot 3600 \cdot S}{\rho \cdot \Delta u \cdot \eta_{str}}
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

