\subsubsection*{3. Volume based on Hydraulics - $V_H$} The Case Study of this thesis applies the general TES implementation framework to a sensible heat storage system including heat pumps and stratified buffer tanks. Since these systems are becoming increasingly popular, $V_H$ is included to illustrate a hydraulic check required for hydraulic TES systems. LTS and TCES require different volume checks depending on the mediums. However, this is outside the scope of this research.
\\

\noindent Formula ($V_H$) ensures the storage unit can physically handle the mass flow rates required by the emitters without failing to meet instantaneous demand:
\\

\begin{equation}
V_H = \frac{q_{net} \cdot \tau_{min}}{\eta}
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $V_H$ is the required hydraulic buffer volume [$m^3$].
    \item $q_{net}$: The peak instantaneous flow mismatch between the secondary distribution circuit and the primary generation circuit [$m^3/h$].
    \item $\tau_{min}$: The hydraulic stability constant, representing the minimum duration required for the system controls to stabilize during a flow transition (typically $0.25$ h).
    \item $\eta$: The volumetric utilization factor, representing the fraction of the storage medium effectively accessible for thermal exchange (typically $0.8$ for stratified water tanks).
\end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent The flow low mismatch is defined as the maximum absolute difference between the total secondary distribution demand and the primary generation supply across the simulation time-series:
\\

\begin{equation}
    q_{net} = \max \left| \sum_{i=1}^{n} q_{sec, i}(t) - q_{pri}(t) \right|
\end{equation}
\\

Where: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item $q_{pri}$ is the primary volumetric flow rate provided by the heat source (e.g., heat pump) during the specified operational period [$m^3/h$].
    \item $q_{sec, i}$ is the required secondary volumetric flow rate for each individual zone $i$ to satisfy the instantaneous thermal demand [$m^3/h$].
\end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent The required flow rate for each individual zone $i$ is calculated based on the thermal demand $Q_{zone,i}$ (from the $Q_{zonal}$ dataset) and the target temperature differential of the emitter(s) of the zone ($\Delta T_i$:
\\

\begin{equation}
    q_{sec, i} = \frac{Q_{zone, i} \cdot 3600}{\rho \cdot c_p \cdot \Delta T_{i}}
\end{equation}
\\

Where: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Q_{zone, i}$ is the heat demand of zone $i$ [$kW$].
    \item $3600$ is the conversion factor from seconds to hours.
    \item $\rho$ is the density of the heat transfer fluid [$\approx 1000 \, kg/m^3$ for water].
    \item $c_p$ is the specific heat capacity of the fluid [$\approx 4.18 \, kJ/kg \cdot K$ for water].
    \item $\Delta T_{i}$ is the design temperature difference for the secondary circuit [$K$].
\end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent The primary flow rate provided by the heat source is defined as:
\\

\begin{equation}
    q_{pri} = \frac{Q_{supply} \cdot 3600}{\rho \cdot c_p \cdot \Delta T_{pri}}
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Q_{supply}$ is the actual thermal power output from the primary heat source [$kW$].
    \item $\Delta T_{pri}$ is the temperature lift across the primary generator (Supply - Return) [$K$].
\end{itemize}
\medskip

