\subsubsection{Approach Motivation}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Strategic compliance} ($V_S$): While dynamic data in $V_C$ fluctuates with weather and other variables, $V_S$ is a quasi-static calculation based on a fixed design objective (e.g., a 4-hour peak-shifting window). It ensures the system meets certain obligations or grid-flexibility policies that must remain valid regardless of whether a specific year was mild or extreme influencing the demand/supply data. This approach establishes a "safety floor" for the storage capacity. 
    \item \textbf{Continuity and SoC Integrity} ($V_C$): This represents the "continuous reality check". Unlike $V_S$, which assumes a fully charged TES for a single event, $V_C$ evaluates the system over a continuous time-series. It accounts for the State of Charge (SoC) over multiple days for example. This is critical for identifying the cumulative failure, where a system successfully manages a single peak but fails on subsequent days because the available supply (grid-constrained or weather-dependent) was insufficient to fully recharge the medium.
    \item \textbf{Hydraulic and kinetic integrity} ($V_H$): This represents the "power-to-energy limit". While $V_S$ and $V_C$ focus on total capacity (kWh), $V_H$ ensures that the TES is physically dimensioned to handle peak instantaneous power and mass flow rates ($q_{net}$). Without this constraint, a system might be energetically sufficient but fail to meet demand because the rate of heat exchange is physically limited. For sensible systems, this prevents the destruction of stratification. For latent and thermochemical systems, it ensures sufficient heat-transfer surface area and reaction (kinetics) to maintain the required output temperature under peak flow conditions.
\end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent The final capacity of a TES system is determined by the intersection of two distinct functional requirements: energy capacity ($V_C$) and operational stability ($V_H$). The energy volume ($V_C$) is a derivative of the thermal demand dataset time-step $dt$, ensuring the medium can meet power demands over a specific duration without exhausting its thermal reserve. 
\\

\noindent Conversely, the stability volume $V_H$ is governed by the hydraulic or mechanical response time $\tau_{min}$ required to protect the primary heat source during rapid load transitions. Depending on the specific control strategy and the sensitivity of the equipment, the total system size is typically established either as the maximum of these two values (\textit{function 12}) (assuming the larger volume can absorb short-term fluctuations) or as their sum (\textit{function 13}) to guarantee a dedicated physical buffer remains intact regardless of the state of charge of the TES system. 

