\subsubsection*{Thermal Dataset}
The thermal demand is integrated as a zonal dataset covering a defined series of time-steps, where positive values ($+$) suggest heating and negative values ($-$) suggest cooling.
\\

\noindent While the raw input is quasi-static, meaning the load profile is pre-calculated and fixed, the simulation engine dynamically corrects this demand in real-time. By applying a thermal mass constant ($M_{zone}$) derived from the structural design and floor area, the model simulates the building's ability to absorb or release energy. This effectively decouples the immediate building needs from the heat source, allowing the structural mass to (partially) bridge supply-demand gaps during grid-congested events.
\\

\noindent Furthermore, directly linking the simulation engine to an external thermal demand model (if possible) enables a fully dynamic exchange. In this configuration, the control logic and the TES state of charge actively influence the building's thermal behaviour in real-time, moving beyond the constraints of a fixed dataset. Moreover, linking the external software or multi-zone model script allows running simulations with different parameters, able to optimize systems and validate its robustness.
