\subsubsection*{Schematic A: Theatre} The theatre hall is served by a dedicated Air Handling Unit (AHU) that conditions a mixture of outside air and recirculated, heat-recovered, zone air to meet the specific ventilation and thermal requirements of this high-occupancy space. This configuration operates independently of the building's central atrium, focussing on converting the external ambient (outdoor) air conditions and the internal set-point using a high-capacity system.

\subsubsection*{Schematic B: Cascaded Strategy} Secondary zones utilize a cascaded air strategy where the building atrium serves as a thermal buffer and primary intake source for Fan Coil Units (FCU) and Variable Air Volume (VAV) units. In this setup, the air in the atrium is pre-conditioned by a second AHU and an underfloor heating (UFH) and cooling system. Before this air reaches other zones, the temperature gap is effectively narrowed between incoming air and the zone's target temperature. This pre-conditioned air reduces the temperature lift required from the water-to-air coils in the FCU and VAV units. As a result, the total kilowatt demand is decreased during grid congested periods when the heat pump modulates down and the TES tank has to bridge this period and deliver the kilowatt demand to the zones.

