\noindent The relationship between real power ($P$) and apparent power ($S$) is defined as:
\\

\begin{equation}
    P = S \cdot PF
\end{equation}

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $P$: Real Power ($kW$)
    \item $S$: Apparent Power ($kVA$)
    \item $PF$: Power Factor (dimensionless ratio between $0$ and $1.0$)
\end{itemize}
\\

\noindent Conventional design peak calculations suggest that the power allocation for the cluster \textit{Tiuri + INS} (\textit{see figure 42: yellow zones and third column}) exceeds the available 55 kVA. Specifically, when the building's mechanical installations and the two all-electric heat pumps are both operating at their estimated design maximums, the cumulative load reaches 81 kVA. Resolving this would require a permanent derating of either the installations or the heat source, potentially compromising climate comfort or heating capacity.
\\

\noindent However, this "static" problem assumes a simultaneous peak that rarely occurs in reality. By utilizing a dynamic climate design, the building can leverage the Atrium as a fresh-air reservoir, allowing the Air Handling Units (AHU) and other mechanical installations to modulate based on actual occupancy and air quality. This allows for coordinated capacity sharing, where the heat source utilizes the residual electrical power left by the mechanical installations. During periods of low occupancy or nighttime setback, the electrical capacity freed up by the installations becomes available for the heat pumps to charge the Thermal Energy Storage (TES) system.
\\

\noindent To quantify this interaction, the instantaneous power available for the heat pump is no longer treated as a fixed value, but as the residual capacity of the grid connection at any given time-step $t$:
\\

\begin{equation}
    P_{\text{HP\_available}}(t) = P_{\text{total\_limit}} - P_{\text{installations}}(t)
\end{equation}
\\

\noindent For the case study, this formula translates to:
\\

\begin{equation}
    P_{\text{HP,available}}(t) = P_{\text{limit}} - \sum \left( P_{\text{light}}(t) + P_{\text{equip}}(t) + P_{\text{fans}}(t) + P_{\text{pumps}}(t) \right)
\end{equation}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent The available heat pump power is based on the operational scenario that the installations are completely off when there is no event. In case the installations (AHU) are modulated down, the available apparent heat pump power reduces slightly. Calculating the thermal output of the heat pump is dependent of the Coefficient of Performance (COP) and can be calculated according to the following formula:
\\

\begin{equation}
    Q_{HP,max}(t) = P_{HP\_available}(t) \cdot COP
\end{equation}
