\subsection{Heat Pump as Heat Source} The initial climate design for the case study equips two all-electric variable-speed heat pumps: one dedicated to cooling, and one dedicated to heating. These heat pumps are air-to-water heat pumps, meaning they extract thermal energy from the outdoor ambient air and transfer it to a hydronic distribution system to heat or cool the building's water-based storage and emitters.


% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Variable-speed Heat Pump} Currently, variable-speed (modulating) heat pumps account for the vast majority of heat pumps on the market due to their superior efficiency and comfort compared to single-speed heat pumps. A single speed heat pump is only able to operate in two modes: on or off. 
\\

\noindent \textit{“When your heat pump is a modulating one, it means that it can work at 100 percent of its capacity, but also at 70 percent or sometimes even 30 percent. This means that if your building has only a small demand, it can switch on at a much lower power, following the demand of the building more accurately. Therefore, modulating heat pumps have fewer start stops, lower wear on the compressor and are in general more quiet. When a modulating heat pump is working in a part-load regime (that is, at a lower than maximum capacity) there is another great benefit. Since now, strictly speaking, the internal components of the heat pump are oversized relative to the heating demand, its efficiency is also higher. Therefore, modulating heat pumps tend to have a higher efficiency than on-off ones”} (Peere, 2025).

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Types of Variable-speed Heat Pumps} Among modulating heat pumps, another distinction can be made regarding the ability to handle thermal loads sequentially versus simultaneously. While standard heat pumps are designed to provide either heating or cooling in a single cycle, specialized heat pumps allow for the concurrent delivery of both. The following section discusses these two types of heat pumps and discuss which option is chosen for the case study.

\subsubsection*{2-pipe Heat Pump} 2-pipe heat pumps can only provide either heating or cooling during a cycle. Most 2-pipe heat pumps are reversible, meaning these units can switch roles of the evaporator and condenser, providing heating in winter and active cooling in summer. Switching from cooling to heating (or vice versa) requires the entire volume of water in the pipes to be tempered to the new target, which creates a "lag" time. 
\\

\noindent The limitation that a single 2-pipe heat pump cannot handle conflicting demands at the same time requires additional operation control strategy: in order to determine the operating mode of the heat pump, a comparison-based decision between heating and cooling demand must be made. A common strategy is switching the heat pump to a mode that is required the most based on the thermal demand. 
\\

\noindent Because the case study does requires simultaneous cooling and heating, and the chosen TES system does not operate well with a single heat pump that switches between heating and cooling mode, the case study has one 2-pipe heat pump dedicated to cooling and one 2-pipe heat pump for heating, omitting the need for a switching strategy.

\subsubsection*{4-pipe Heat Pump} Ideally, a 4-pipe heat pump is installed in a building that requires both heating and cooling simultaneously, like in a performing arts center. This saves the need for two separate heat pumps. However, the choice for two 2-pipe heat pumps instead of one 4-pipe heat pump is part of the boundary conditions of the case study. 
\\

\noindent A 4-pipe system utilizes two independent circuits: one dedicated to chilled water and one to hot water, both available to be sent to emitters simultaneously, or to two separate buffer tanks. This system's highest efficiency occurs during balanced loads. The heat extracted from a zone requiring cooling is recycled and pumped into the heating circuit instead of being exhausted to the atmosphere (Brent, 2024). 
\\

