\subsubsection{Heat Pump Capacity} Both of the heat pumps of the case study have an initial rated power of 75 kW. When total building demand consistently exceeds the peak capacity of the heat source, the system operates beyond its steady-state limits. During periods of grid congestion, the buffer tank functions as the primary energy source to bridge the deficit. However, if this instantaneous peak load is sustained, the finite storage capacity is eventually depleted, leading to a system failure regardless of grid availability. This indicates that the heat source is undersized for the building's absolute peak requirements. In that case, the rated thermal power should be higher of the chosen heat pump. The final simulation will reveal if these rated capacities are sufficient enough. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Coefficient of Performance} The efficiency of the energy conversion process in a heat pump is measured by the Coefficient of Performance (COP), which represents the relationship between the heat energy produced and the electrical power consumed. The COP is be defined as the ratio of thermal heat delivered (output) to the electrical power input, and is calculated by:

\begin{equation}
    COP = \frac{Q_{hp}}{P_{hp}}
\end{equation}
\\

Where $Q_{hp}$ denotes the heat output and $P_{hp}$ represents the actual electrical input ($P_{HP,available}(t)$).

\subsubsection*{COP Carnot} The COP Carnot represents the theoretical maximum efficiency of a heat pump, defined by the temperatures of the heat source and heat sink ($T_{source}$ and $T_{sink}$). It assumes a perfectly a perfectly reversible cycle with no energy losses, providing the maximum ceiling of performance for any given temperature lift:
\\

\begin{equation}
    COP_{Carnot} = \frac{T_{sink}}{T_{sink} - T_{source}}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection*{COP Real} The COP Real (or $COP_{actual}$) is the performance actually achieved by the hardware after accounting for mechanical inefficiencies and thermal energy losses. In energy modelling, the COP real is typically derived by multiplying the Carnot limit by the Lorentz factor ($\eta_{Lo}$), which for air-source heat pumps in the Netherlands is often estimated at 0.5 to reflect real-world operation (De Kleijn Energy Consulting, n.d.):
\\

\begin{equation}
        COP_{real, heating} = \eta_{Lo} \times \frac{T_{sink}}{T_{sink} - T_{amb}}
\end{equation}
\\

\begin{equation}
        COP_{real, cooling} = \eta_{Lo} \times \frac{T_{return}}{T_{amb} - T_{return}}
\end{equation}
\\

Where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $T_{sink}$: Supply temperature to the emission system [K].
\item $T_{return}$: Return water temperature from the emission system [K].
\item $T_{amb}$: Ambient outdoor air temperature [K].
\item $\eta_{Lo}$: Lorentz efficiency factor (typically 0.5 – 0.7).
\end{itemize}

