\subsection{Thermal Demand Data} The general TES implementation framework suggests two distinct pathways for acquiring thermal demand profiles of the building as a whole and for the separate zones. The case study building has been simulated in the external software called \textit{Vabi Elements}. This software has a specialized environment for calculating hourly thermal loads, energy labels, and indoor climate performance. One disadvantage of using this software to obtain the thermal demand datasets per zone is that the software is a black box for its user. 
\\

\noindent Therefore, this thesis has modelled the case study in the simplified energy balance model in Python: a multi-zone, first-order lumped capacitance model based on the hourly energy balance method of the ISO 13790 / NEN-EN-ISO 52016-1 (\textit{see Appendix C}). Even though this model is may be less accurate than external simulation models, it allows the users to insert their own building in this model and set a list of variables that can be adjusted based on user preferences. This multi-zone lumped capacitance model also allows the users to track all heat flows per zone per time-step to get a better understanding of the dynamic energy balance. Moreover, this multi-zone model can later be linked to the TES simulation, allowing for a range of optimisation and validation possibilities. 

\subsubsection{Transient Multi-zone First-order Lumped Capacitance Model} The following section briefly explains the multi-zone model and its most important components. However, to see the full logic and equations behind the simulation model, see the appendix. 

\subsubsection*{Lumped Capacitance and Thermal Mass} The thermal behaviour of the building is modelled using a multi-zone, first-order lumped capacitance approach based on the hourly energy balance method prescribed by ISO 52016-1. In this framework, each thermal area of the building is categorised into climatization zones where the combined heat storage capacity of the air, furniture, and internal construction layers is "lumped" into a single thermal mass node. This simplification allows the model to treat each zone as a thermal battery, capturing the physical inertia of the building's structure, such as the high specific capacity of heavy solid concrete floors and walls.

\subsubsection*{Hourly Energy Balance and Inter-zonal Coupling} The model calculates hourly energy demands by performing a continuous heat balance that accounts for transmission through the building envelope, air infiltration, and controlled ventilation. Internal heat gains from occupants and equipment, along with solar radiation through glazing and opaque facade and roof, are balanced against these losses every hour. Importantly, as a multi-zone network, the simulation calculates the inter-zonal heat exchange across shared internal walls and floors. This link enables the simulation of cascaded air strategies, where the pre-conditioned air of a buffer zone, such as an atrium, influences the temperature of adjacent secondary zones. A simplified formula capturing this hourly balance is defined as follows:
\\

\begin{equation}
    Q(t) = [ H_{tot} \cdot (\theta_{int} - \theta_{ext}(t)) ] - [ Q_{int}(t) + Q_{sol}(t) + Q_{stor}(t) +  Q_{ext\_flow}(t) ]
\end{equation}

Where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Q(t)$ is the net thermal demand at hour $t$ [kWh].
    \item $H_{tot}$ is the total heat loss coefficient, including transmission, infiltration, and ventilation [kW/K].
    \item $\theta_{int}$ is the target internal set-point temperature [${}^\circ$C].
    \item $\theta_{ext}(t)$ is the ambient outdoor temperature at hour $t$ [${}^\circ$C].
    \item $Q_{int}(t)$ represents internal gains from occupants and appliances [kWh].
    \item $Q_{sol}(t)$ represents solar heat gains through glazing and opaque surfaces [kWh].
    \item $Q_{stor}(t)$ is the thermal energy stored or released by the building's thermal mass [kWh].
    \item $Q_{ext\_flow}(t)$ is the heat exchanged with adjacent zones via shared internal boundaries [kWh].
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Transient Temperature Calculation and Demand Determination} To determine the hourly heating and cooling loads, the simulation employs an exponential solution to solve the transient heat equation. For every time-step, the model identifies an equilibrium temperature, which is the theoretical point the room would reach if external conditions were static. Because the building’s thermal mass prevents it from reaching this equilibrium instantly, the exponential formula calculates the actual room temperature transition over each time-step (60-minute) interval. If the resulting "free-floating" temperature falls outside predefined comfort set-points, the model calculates the exact kilowatt demand required from the HVAC system to maintain the target temperature.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Schematic Overview} This multi-zone simulation model uses a "Master and Worker" architecture (\textit{see Appendix D}) to calculate the annual thermal energy demand of a building across 8760 hours. The Orchestrator acts as the master script, managing global weather data, inter-zone heat transfer through shared internal walls and floors, and cascaded ventilation strategies where the Atrium acts as a buffer zone. Within this loop, each individual zone is treated as an instance of the \textit{ZoneModel} class, which functions as the physics engine to calculate hourly heat gains and losses, thermal mass effects, and the mechanical energy needed to meet temperature set-points. The process concludes by aggregating these hourly results into comprehensive annual heating and cooling totals for both individual zones and the entire building. 
