\subsubsection{Results: Multi-zone Model with Temperature Set-point Ranges} The model utilizes a hierarchy of temperature thresholds including heating, ventilation, and cooling set-points, to optimize energy use by maximizing the periods when the building can free-float. The Orchestrator defines these set-points through weekly profiles that transition between high-precision "Comfort" targets during occupied hours and wider "Setback" ranges during nights and weekends. Within the physics engine, active HVAC is only engaged if the zone temperature falls below the heating set-point or rises above the cooling set-point. Between these limits, the model first attempts to maintain comfort via the ventilation set-point, which triggers low-energy natural cooling (like opening windows or using a bypass) whenever external conditions are favourable. 
\\

\noindent By allowing the temperature to drift within this multi-layered deadband, the model leverages the building's thermal mass to absorb internal and solar gains, effectively delaying or entirely mitigating the need for active mechanical systems. This is the method results in a realistic demand profile for the case study.
\\

