\subsubsection{Results: Multi-zone Model with Fixed Temperature Set-points} By setting the heating, cooling, and ventilation set-points to a single, identical value, the model effectively eliminates the "deadband" and prevents the zones from free-floating. In this configuration, the physics engine forces the indoor temperature to remain static across every time-step, meaning the resulting demand reflects the absolute energy required to perfectly neutralize all solar gains, internal heat, and transmission losses to maintain that exact temperature. 
\\

\noindent Since the temperature is no longer permitted to drift, the building’s thermal mass is not actively used within the class logic to buffer shifts; instead, the model outputs a "raw" hourly energy profile. This provides a clean baseline demand for your TES script, which can then apply its own thermal storage strategy to this load while ensuring the buffering work of the building's mass is only calculated once in the downstream logic. 
\\

\noindent In hindsight, a live connection between the multi-zone model and the Simulation Engine would allow the user to define temperature dead band zones and integrate natural ventilation. Rather than defining the allowed drift temperatures inside the Simulation Engine, the multi-zone model could more accurately calculate demands outside of these comfort boundaries, and feed this thermal demand and the updated zone temperatures into the Simulation Engine, describing a constant exchange of information per time-step. Currently, the connection is purely sequential, where the Simulation Engine calculates the new temperatures and corrected thermal demands by itself, although less accurate. This is further discussed in the limitations and further research recommendations section of this thesis.

