\subsubsection {Hydronic Piping of Stratified Buffer Tank} Connecting a heat pump (HP) to a sensible heat storage tank is a critical design choice that dictates system efficiency, thermal stratification, and operational flexibility. The integration typically follows one of three primary piping architectures, each offering a different trade-off between hydraulic complexity and thermal performance. These piping variations are not to be confused with the 2-pipe versus 4-pipe heat pump types discussed in section 4.2.2; while the latter refers to the machine's ability to generate heating and cooling, these tank-side variations represent the primary hydraulic interaction between the heat source (heat pump), the storage volume, and the zone emitters. These configurations determine the fluid dynamics between the heat pump, the storage (tank) volume, and emitters, specifically governing how mass flow is managed to preserve thermal stratification and achieve hydraulic independence. 

