\subsubsection*{4-pipe Tank Configuration (\textit{Parallel})} For a multi-zone building with $n$ zones, heterogeneous emitters, with varying $\Delta T$, the 4-pipe parallel configuration is the most robust architecture and is therefore chosen for the case study. By providing total hydraulic and thermal decoupling, it ensures the system remains functional regardless of varying zone requirements or grid-induced modulation (Siegenthaler, 2024). This setup is comprised of two loops: primary loop (ASHP and tank), and the secondary loop (tank and zones). A secondary system offers superior efficiency and flexibility, particularly in projects with multiple zones with varying heating/cooling loads (Plastpump, n.d.).
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Hydraulic decoupling}: Isolates the ASHP from variable flow rates of $n$ zones, preventing "low-flow" errors and protecting the compressor.
    \item \textbf{Thermal layering}: Utilizes natural buoyancy to manage differing return temperatures, preserving high-grade heat at the top of the tank to serve as a reliable "thermal battery" during grid congestion.
    \item \textbf{Operational flexibility}: Supports mixed-emitter scenarios, allowing high-temperature zones (e.g., fan coils) and low-temperature zones (e.g., floor heating) to coexist without thermal interference. 
\end{itemize}
        
\subsubsection*{2-pipe Tank Configuration (\textit{Series})} This setup is less suitable due to hydraulic risk. In a multi-zone system, the ASHP becomes very dependent on zone flow. If multiple zones close, the resulting flow drop can trigger high-pressure faults and system shutdowns. Furthermore, it lacks the storage exergy needed for long-term grid-load shifting. This setup is more suitable for a single-zone configuration.

\subsubsection*{3-pipe Tank Configuration (\textit{Hybrid})} This setup is less suitable due to thermal dilution. The shared return pipe (manifold) forces return water from all $n$ zones into a single entry point. This creates significant internal turbulence and "smears" the thermocline, quickly degrading the tank's thermal stratification and reducing the autonomy of the building during grid congested periods. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Stratification Maintenance} Thermal stratification in parallel buffer configurations is fundamentally dependent on the maintenance of a stable thermocline. When inlet velocities are low, gravitational buoyancy successfully separates water by density, preserving the high-grade thermal energy required for the emission system. Excessive flow velocities trigger turbulent mixing within the tank, 'smearing' the temperature gradient. This prevents the maintenance of distinct hot and cold layers. As a result, the temperature inside the tank drops to a lukewarm level. While the total amount of energy remains the same, the water is no longer hot enough to heat the building effectively (loss of exergy). 
\\

\noindent Sizing for low velocity, flow matching, diffusers and internal baffles, strategically placed sensors monitoring the temperature, and proper height-to-width ratio of tank mitigate the loss of stratification inside the tank. The stratification efficiency factor ($\eta_{str}$) accounts for how much extra volume is needed to compensate for these effects.
\\

\noindent Additionally, this thesis implements a minimum modulation threshold for the heat pumps to preserve thermal stratification within the storage tanks. During grid congestion or periods of zero thermal demand, this threshold ensures that the heat pumps do not operate at sub-optimal power levels, which would result in a low temperature lift and cause the thermocline to smear through convective mixing. By reserving a small, dedicated electrical power floor for both heat pumps, the system ensures that any energy injected into the tanks maintains the necessary buoyancy to support a distinct thermal gradient at all times. This prevents the tanks from drifting too far from the desired temperatures. 

