\subsubsection{Climate System Piping} The building's hydronic architecture employs a hybrid distribution strategy where high-capacity Air Handling Units (AHUs utilize a four-pipe configuration for instantaneous mode switching, while Fan Coil Units (FCUs) and Underfloor heating (UFH) are constrained by a two-pipe seasonal changeover logic. This physical limitation creates a significant discrepancy with the TES sizing algorithm, which assumes four-pipe agility and instantaneous response across all terminal units. 
\\

\noindent To align this physical piping infrastructure with the simulation's logic, each zone and its emitter is modelled as a 4-pipe system. This set-up is the most industry standard, though more expensive and complex to construct. Even in a 2-pipe system, usually the thermal demand per season consists of either cooling or heating, effectively minimizing their demand variability within a short timespan.
