\subsubsection{Integration of Renewable Energy} The case study includes the addition of PV panels on the roof. This extra generation of electricity increases the total electrical power for those time-steps, alleviating some of the grid congested periods. The available thermal capacity of the ASHP during grid congested periods (events) can be calculated according to the following formula, where the solar energy is converted to thermal energy [kW] per time step:
\\

\begin{equation}
    \dot{Q}_{HP,max}(t) = \min \left( Q_{cap}, \left[ (Grid\_Limit\_kVA(t) + PV\_kVA(t)) \times PF \right] \times COP(t) \right)
\end{equation}
\\

Where: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Q_{cap}$ = ASHP rated thermal capacity [kW].
    \item $PV\_kVA(t)$ = Apparent power generated by photovoltaic panels [kVA].
    \item $Grid\_Limit\_kVA(t)$ = Electrical input limited during grid congestion (event) [kVA].
    \item Power Factor (PF) = 0.9 - 0.95
    \item COP = Coefficient of Performance of the heat pump at time ($i$).
\end{itemize}
\medskip

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{PV case study.pdf}
    
    % This command forces the caption to be small only for THIS figure
    \captionsetup{font=footnotesize} 
    
    \caption{Photovoltaic panels integration with instance of $Q_{cap}$ = 75 kW.}
    \label{fig:stratification_comparison}
\end{figure} 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent To determine the heat pump's thermal output, $Q_{source,t}$, the PV panels' apparent power must be estimated at each hourly time-step to quantify the total electrical energy available for heat pump operation. Given the available data regarding the PV panels, location, and solar irradiance, the following formula's are used to calculate $PV\_kVA_i$: 
\\

\noindent 1. The Solar Irradiance Formula (Isotropic Sky Model) ($I_{poa,t}$) calculates the \textit{Plane of Array} irradiance (Perry, 2018) per time-step. It tilts the horizontal solar data from the climate dataset to the specific $\cos(7^\circ)$ angle of the PV panels of the case study:
\\

\begin{equation}
I_{\text{poa}}(t) = (GG(t) - GD(t)) \cdot \cos(7^\circ) + GD(t) \cdot \frac{1 + \cos(7^\circ)}{2}
\end{equation}
\\

Where: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item GG is the Global Horizontal Irradiance adapted from NEN5060-B2 climate dataset.
    \item GD is the Diffuse Horizontal Irradiance adapted from NEN5060-B2 climate dataset.
    \item Tilt in $\cos(7^\circ)$ is the angle of the PV panels. \textit{Note: While the tilt is defined as $7^\circ$, trigonometric functions in computational environments typically require this value to be converted to radians ($0.122$ rad) to return the correct cosine ratio.}
\end{itemize}
\medskip

