\subsection{Control Strategy} The effectiveness of a TES system is largely determined by the underlying control logic, which dictates how and when energy is stored or released. For this case study, a priority-based strategy is used to stay within the 61.25 kVA grid limit while maintaining occupant comfort. 
\\

\noindent The control strategy identifies two systems: the TES system entails the heat pump and stratified buffer tank, and a separate control strategy for the UFH, which is not an active component in the TES Simulation Engine, but part of the Multi-zone thermal model. Therefore, this section also includes the control strategy for the UFH in the Multi-zone model since its effects will influence the thermal demand dataset that is eventually used as input for the TES Simulation Engine. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{TES Charging Strategy} When auxiliary installations are inactive and the heat pup has electrical headroom, the system follows a hierarchical (re)charging sequence. This sequence is dynamically adjusted based on the available time between high thermal load events:
\\

\noindent \textbf{Scenario A: Sufficient recovery window ($\Delta t_{\text{window}} \geq t_{\text{sat},struct}$)}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Primary priority: set-point maintenance}: The heat pump first satisfies the instantaneous heating demand of each zone to maintain the baseline comfort temperature ($T_{set}$).
    \item \textbf{Secondary priority: structural pre-charging}. Any residual surplus power is utilized to charge the building's thermal mass, raising the zone temperature toward the maximum allowable limit ($T_{max}$).
    \item \textbf{Tertiary priority: active TES charging}. Only once the building structure is thermally saturated does the system direct surplus energy to charge the water buffer tank to its maximum capacity.
\end{enumerate}
\medskip

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent \textbf{Scenario B: Immediate constraint window ($\Delta t_{\text{window}} < t_{\text{sat},struct}$)}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Primary priority: set-point maintenance}. Instantaneous demand is satisfied first.
    \item \textbf{Secondary priority: active TES charging}. Because the water buffer provides a higher power density, faster response, and is much quicker to recharge, it is prioritized to reach a full State of Charge (SoC) quickly.
    \item \textbf{Tertiary priority: structural pre-charging}. Any remaining energy is diverted to the structural mass, acknowledging that full thermal saturation may not be achieved before the next event.
\end{enumerate}
\medskip

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent Within the structural charging phase, a secondary control layer manages the distribution of energy across multiple zones: 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sequential recharging of zones}: Zones are addressed based on a pre-defined priority list (e.g., Zone 1 (main stage) first, Zone 2, 3, and 4 after). High priority zones are restored to $T_{max}$ (or $T_{set}$) before lower priority zones receive surplus energy.
    \item \textbf{Proportional recharging of zones}: The proportional division would be based on the "thermal hunger" of each zone, as described in the general workflow \textit{Part 4: Control strategy}. This logic ensures that larger zones with higher thermal inertia or zones that have drifted furthest from their set-points receive a mathematically fair share of the available energy. For each zone $i$:
    \\

    $$\text{Hunger}_i = (T_{max,i} - T_{current,i}) \times M_{zone, i}$$   
    
   \&
  
     $$ZoneShare_i = \left( \frac{Hunger_i}{\sum Hunger} \right) \cdot P_{surplus}$$
\end{itemize}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{TES Discharging Strategy} During events when grid availability is restricted or the demand supply outweighs the supply, and the heat pump is forced to modulate down, the system transitions to a hierarchical discharge sequence:
\\

\noindent \textbf{Long-duration event (high inertia focus)}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Primary priority: modulated heat pump output}: The heat pump operates at its maximum constrained capacity ($Q_{HP,max}(t)$, see section \ref{Problem statement} \textit{Problem Statement}) to meet as much demand as possible.
    \item \textbf{Secondary priority: passive structural discharge}. The zones are allowed to drift naturally from $T_{set}$ (or $T_{current}$ when not fully charged) toward the minimum allowed "swing" temperature $T_{min}$. The building's thermal inertia acts as the primary buffer.
    \item \textbf{Tertiary priority: active TES discharge}: The sensible buffer tank acts as a tactical reserve, injecting heat only when the structural temperature threatens to breach the $T_{min}$ threshold. 
    \item \textbf{Deficit tracking}: If the combined capacity of the thermal mass and the tank is exhausted, the resulting unmet demand is logged to determine the required volume of the TES ($V_C$).
\end{enumerate}
\medskip

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent Within the primary priority: \textit{modulated heat pump output}, a secondary control layer manages the distribution of energy across multiple zones: 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sequential HP output division}: Zones are addressed based on a pre-defined priority list (e.g., Zone 1 (main stage) first, Zone 2, 3, and 4 after). High priority zones receive the HP modulated output first to meet try to meet the instantaneous demand before lower priority zones receive output energy (if there is any left).
    \item \textbf{Proportional HP output division}: The proportional division would be based on the thermal demand share of the total modulated heat pump output. For each zone $i$:
    \\
  
     $$ZoneShare_i = \left( \frac{Demand_i}{\sum Demand} \right) \cdot Q_{HP,max}(t)$$
\end{itemize}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{UFH Control Strategy} The primary goal of the Underfloor Heating (UFH) system is to minimize the governing cumulative annual energy deficit ($E_{\text{cum}}$), which acts as the core stress metric representing any heating and/or cooling load that exceeds the heat pump’s dynamic capacity ceiling and must be covered by the storage (buffer tank) system. 
\\

\noindent By utilizing the Atrium’s massive concrete floor slab as a structural thermal battery pre-charged during high-efficiency solar or off-peak windows, the system slowly radiates heat to adjacent zones via internal wall connections. This peak-shifting strategy prevents the premature depletion of the TES buffet tank and directly minimizing the final required physical buffer tank volume ($\text{m}^3$).

\subsubsection*{Fixed versus Dynamic Scheduling} The UFH is part of the multi-zone thermal model. The control strategy must be defined within that simulation. The TES Simulation Engine will only see the required energy demand per time-step for the UFH based on the chosen control strategy. There are two options regarding the control strategy for the UFH: a fixed daily schedule for the entire year, or a dynamic schedule varying from day to day. The following section proposes a stepwise optimisation framework for both strategies:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dynamic capacity boundary mapping}: The optimisation method starts with evaluating the electrical constraints by calculating the real-time building base loads and available grid capacity. Within the Availability Engine, the maximum thermal output potentials for the heat pumps are dynamically determined as an hourly capacity ceiling (based on COP, available kVA headrooms, solar PV yields and power split between the heat pumps). 
    \item \textbf{Thermal fighting penalization}: Multi-zone thermal fighting is explicitly penalized to prevent systemic inefficiencies. For instance, if an aggressive UFH schedule overcharges the atrium (zone 2), excessive heat conducts through shared internal walls into adjacent zones. This could trigger concurrent cooling demands in these adjacent zones. Consequently , the simultaneous cooling load then affects the power split between the heat pumps, if set to \textit{proportional split}, shrinking the heating capacity headroom. Besides a lower heating capacity, the extra cooling demand turns the UFH into a an energetic liability rather than a strategic buffer. Therefore, a severe penalty is imposed on the schedule that causes this situation.
    \item \textbf{Optimisation indicators}: To optimise both schedule control strategies, the hourly capacity deficits are calculated: any hour where the building's total load exceeds the heat pump's capacity ceiling. These deficits are processed through a squared cost function, $\sum (\text{deficit}_t)^2$, which heavily penalizes and flattens large demand spikes, where a linear function would have treated massive deficit spikes the exact same as several tiny, harmless deficits spread over a long time. 
    \\

    \noindent Finally, the physical water tanks are not sized by the total annual sum of these deficits, but by the single worst-case continuous energy deficit window ($E_{\text{max\_gap}}$). Tracked chronologically over all 8,760 hours of the year, this peak depletion sequence reveals the exact moment the heat pump is most overwhelmed, directly dictating the absolute storage volume ($V_c$) required for the hot and cold water tanks.
\end{enumerate}
\\

