\subsubsection*{Strategy Selection} There are three possible strategies for the UFH in the simulations models: no UFH, active UFH with optimised a fixed schedule or active UFH with dynamic a schedule. The selection between the strategies is determined by balancing physical hardware savings and peak-shifting against operational complexity: 

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Physical TES Volume ($\text{m}^3$)}: Each optimized thermal dataset with or without UFH is processed through the optimizer section of the main TES Simulation Engine, to isolate its single worst-case continuous energy deficit window. The UFH strategy that yields the smallest required physical water storage tank wins the primary advantage by minimizing installation costs and structural footprint.
    \item \textbf{Operational complexity}: The Fixed schedule carries zero infrastructure overhead, providing a static daily timeline easily hardcoded into standard building controls. The Rolling Horizon method introduces high operational complexity, requiring continuous internet access for live 24-hour weather forecasts and a local processor to solve a 264-iteration loop every midnight.
    \item \textbf{Performance Margin}: The system defaults to the simpler optimised fixed UFH schedule if the dynamic method yields negligible tank reductions. The Rolling Horizon MPC is only selected if its forecast-adaptive logic achieves a high-value drop in tank volume, proving that its daily flexibility is necessary to handle extreme seasonal anomalies.
\end{enumerate}
\\

\noindent Chapter 8.8 \textit{Simulation model} will experiment with different UFH strategies and see which strategy has the most significant outcome: maintaining occupant thermal comfort under grid congestion- and equipment restraints with the smallest possible stratified buffer tanks. 


\subsubsection*{Heat pump capacity calculation) To determine the heat pump's dynamic capacity ceiling, the system requires the proportional power split. However, that split is directly calculated from the thermal demands of the zone emitters trying to maintain their standard comfort set-points (21C for the office spaces and a 16C to 28C range for the atrium), completely excluding any UFH conditioning inputs. To break this circular loop, the power split curve is decoupled from the controller by using a fixed, weather-driven baseline demand, calculated entirely without the UFH, to establish a stable, unmoving capacity boundary for the optimization.


