\subsubsection*{COP Methodology} The simulation model determines the COP using the Lorentz formula rather than static manufacturer performance curves. While fixed performance curves provide empirical data for specific heat pumps, the Lorentz approach ensures theoretical consistency across a wide temperature range. this method allows the simulation to adapt dynamically to varying source and sink temperatures by calculating the maximum thermodynamic potential of a cycle. \\

\noindent To reflect advanced hardware, the model uses a three-stage cooling logic based on the difference between the outside air temperature ($T_{amb}$) and the chilled water set-point ($T_{source}$ = $10^\circ\text{C}$). A multi-stage heating logic is not necessary since the temperatures in the Netherlands do not exceed those of the supply temperatures for heating throughout the year. 
\\

\noindent Normally, when the outside air is cooler than your cold supply temperature, one would open windows to let in the cold air. However, in case that is not possible due to noise constraints or there not being any windows to open, another method is used: if $T_{amb}$ is lower than $T_{source}$, the system uses a so-called \textit{dry cooler} to bypass the compressor entirely. During this Free Cooling mode, the compressor is turned off. Electrical use is then restricted to the small amount of power needed for fans and pumps, and the cold outside air is used directly for cooling (Delta T Systems, 2025). Because of this, the system reaches an "Equivalent COP" between 10.0 and 20.0. These high values are much better than standard mechanical cooling because the system uses the cold outside air as a natural energy sink. 
\\

