\subsubsection*{Thermal Stability and Hardware Constraints}
The simulation framework incorporates an integrated Availability Engine to regulate the concurrent electrical demands of two separate heat pump units, each dedicated to its own thermal energy storage (TES) tank. To prevent electrical overloading of the building's electricity connection, the total available electrical power, derived from grid capacity and photovoltaic generation, is partitioned based on a user-defined \textit{heating share input}.
\\

\noindent Each unit's control architecture accounts for real-world hardware limitations by enforcing a 1.5 kVA mechanical minimum, representing approximately 5\% of a standard 30 kVA compressor capacity. This floor is critical for thermocline maintenance, ensuring that even during low-demand periods, the heat pumps operate at a baseline level to stabilize the tank's temperature gradient and offset continuous thermal drift (\textit{drift-rate}).
\\

\noindent Any surplus energy generated at this modulation floor is injected into the respective TES or building mass, effectively performing "maintenance" on the thermal state rather than resulting in theoretical wastage.
\\

