\section{Discussion and Conclusions} 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Interpretation of the Results} This section of the discussion will interpret the results from the previous chapter.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{System Decoupling and Capacity Constraints in the Baseline Configuration} The observed independence between heating and cooling capacity requirements suggests that the systems are thermally decoupled under the current baseline. This lack of interaction arises because peak heating and cooling demands occur at different times, meaning the simulation treats the two thermal storage systems as distinct, non-interacting parameters. Consequently, because tank sizing is dictated by the specific window of peak energy deficit for each system, the cooling and heating systems function as parallel, independent variables. Any attempt to optimize the volume of one system yields negligible benefits for the other, confirming that the baseline configuration lacks the cross-system synergy achievable through more integrated control strategies.

\subsubsection*{Heat Map Heating} The sensitivity analysis for the heating system, characterized by a transition at 65 kW and a saturation point at 115 kW, stems from the distribution of available kVA between the heat pumps. At a cooling capacity of 55 kW, simultaneous zone cooling demands prioritize the cooling-dedicated heat pump, which restricts the kVA available for the heating system to manage its thermal deficit. The plateau observed after 115 kW indicates a structural limit; beyond this capacity, the limiting factor for storage optimization is not heat pump performance, but the total available kVA during the period of the largest cumulative energy deficit.

\subsubsection*{Heat Map Cooling} The interaction between heating capacity and cooling requirements presents a reciprocal relationship to the heating analysis. The change at 65 kW demonstrates that the cooling heat pump can independently handle peak cooling demands once it reaches a sufficient power threshold. Furthermore, even when the thermal deficit is resolved, a specific cold tank volume is still required to satisfy the hydraulic stability check. This confirms that the final tank size is not determined by thermal needs alone, as it must also account for the hydraulic requirements of the system.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Integrated UFH Strategy} In this scenario, heating capacity is the dominant design factor. Increasing capacity significantly reduces the peak energy deficit ($E_{max,gap}$) during high-demand periods, allowing the system to bridge supply-demand mismatches more efficiently. By holding cooling capacity constant, the influence of the fixed and dynamic schedules on heating storage requirements is isolated, revealing how each strategy impacts overall performance.

\subsubsection*{Sensitivity and Capacity Thresholds} The sharp reduction in storage requirements occurs because higher heating capacities decrease the peak energy deficit. As heating capacity rises, the time window of the supply-demand mismatch shrinks, allowing the heat pump to recover and recharge the thermal energy storage tank more rapidly. The 85 kW knee point identifies the threshold of diminishing returns for capacity investment, while the further reduction observed at 105 kW demonstrates how additional capacity can bring the storage system within realistic sizing constraints.

\subsubsection*{Saturation and Scheduling Performance} The plateau observed beyond 115 kW marks a saturation point where the system's heating capacity becomes the primary driver for neutralizing thermal deficits, rather than the scheduling strategy. At these higher capacity levels, the physical ability of the heat pump to meet demand overwhelms the potential gains from scheduling, rendering further storage optimization negligible.
\\

\noindent The dynamic strategy underperforms relative to the fixed schedule in this regard. This is due to internal constraints within the predictive model, which limit the dynamic framework's ability to exploit the building's thermal mass as effectively as the structured, pre-defined fixed schedule.

\subsubsection*{Marginal Optimization} The 95 kW configuration serves as an optimal design point when balancing capital costs against storage footprint. However, if the primary objective is to minimize the physical footprint, the 105 kW configuration is the preferred choice, as it achieves the lowest practical storage requirement before reaching the point of diminishing returns. In all cases, the reliance on $V_H$ for cold tank sizing demonstrates that the final design is constrained by hydraulic system stability rather than purely by the thermal cooling deficit.
\\

\noindent For the dynamic UFH schedule, the stagnation in volume reduction at 150 kW suggests a similar structural limit. Ultimately, the available kVA during the period of maximum thermal deficit becomes the limiting factor for storage optimization rather than the maximum potential heat pump capacity. This confirms that even under a dynamic MPC framework, storage requirements are fundamentally constrained by the interaction between hydraulic stability and peak power availability.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{UFH Strategy Selection: Predictability versus Adaptability} The comparison between control strategies highlights a fundamental trade-off between predictability and adaptability. The fixed strategy prioritizes static stability and minimizes $E_{max,gap}$ by optimizing for a worst-case scenario. This results in smaller required tank volumes because the system follows a rigid, repeatable profile.
\\

\noindent In contrast, the dynamic strategy prioritizes operational agility and daily performance. While this increases the storage volume requirement compared to the fixed approach, it allows the system to respond to real-time fluctuations rather than relying on a singular statistical worst-case. The performance difference suggests that the dynamic MPC is tuned to optimize for energy efficiency and operational flexibility, whereas the fixed schedule is more effective at minimizing $E_{max,gap}$ and the associated physical storage capacity. The subsequent analysis examines specific time-steps to demonstrate how these strategies differ in operation at a more detailed scale.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Analysis of Peak Energy Deficit Windows} The implementation of active underfloor heating managed by Model Predictive Control, combined with thermal energy storage and increased heat pump capacity, effectively redistributes thermal loads. By dynamically shifting demand, the integrated system resolves energy deficits that the baseline configuration cannot satisfy. These findings confirm that the primary benefit of the integrated approach is its ability to bridge supply-demand gaps that would otherwise cause the system to violate thermal comfort constraints.

\subsubsection*{Heating and Cooling Deficit Dynamics} The system manages thermal energy flow by balancing temporal windows against structural saturation constraints ($\Delta t_{window} \lessgtr \Delta t_{sat,struct}$).
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Heating Deficit}: When the dynamic MPC demand curve falls below the heating supply ceiling, supply exceeds demand. The system then initiates either a tank recharge or returns the building zones to the set-point temperature. This mechanism ensures comfort while preventing the tank from reaching a state of total, unrecoverable depletion.
    \item \textbf{Cooling Deficit}: Because cooling demands are represented as negative values, the demand curve appears visually above the cooling supply capacity when a cooling surplus exists. During these intervals, the system similarly recharges the tank or returns zones to the set-point temperature.
\end{itemize}
\medskip

\noindent This complementary relationship between demand and supply capacity confirms how the integrated system maintains thermal balance, even when cooling and heating requirements fluctuate significantly throughout the year.

\subsubsection {Final TES Volumes} The determination of the total tank capacity relies on whether the volumes for thermal storage ($V_C$) and the hydraulic stability (($V_H$) are treated as a combined functional requirement or as mutually exclusive safety margins. In a "maximum" sizing approach for the selected scenario of the dynamic UFH strategy (with heating max capacity: 115 kW and cooling max capacity: 65 kW):
\\

\begin{equation}
    V_{final, heating} = \max \left(V_C = 2.55 m^3 , V_H = 0.47 m^3\right)
\end{equation}
\\

\begin{equation}
    V_{final, cooling} = \max \left(V_C = 3.63 m^3 , V_H = 2.73 m^3\right)
\end{equation}
\\

\noindent the larger of the two values is selected under the assumption that the substantial volume required for long-term energy storage naturally encompasses the smaller volume needed for short-term control stability. For this logic to remain physically sound, the system's control sensors must be positioned to ensure that even when the storage capacity is being utilized, a residual buffer zone equivalent to $V_H$ remains available at the bottom of the tank to protect the heat pump during sudden flow transitions.
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent Conversely, a "sum" approach where:
\\

\begin{equation}
    V_{final, heating} = 2.55 m^3 +  0.47 m^3 = 3.02 m^3
\end{equation}
\\

\begin{equation}
    V_{final, cooling} = 3.63 m^3 + 2.73 m^3 = 6.36 m^3
\end{equation}
\\

\noindent is often utilized in high-reliability applications to prevent thermocline "washout" during periods of maximum thermal saturation. While $V_C$ defines the capacity required for discharge during grid congestion, a vessel sized exclusively for this energy volume risks reaching a state of total high-temperature saturation at the conclusion of a charging cycle. Should a hydraulic transition occur at this peak (where building demand decreases further while the heat pump maintains a high-output charging mode), the absence of a residual cold-water zone would cause the immediate ingestion of high-temperature return water, resulting in so-called "short cycling" leading to mechanical stress. By adding $V_H$ as a dedicated backup volume, the 15-minute $\tau_{min}$ stability period is preserved, ensuring the heat pump can modulate safely even when the active energy storage ($V_C$) is fully exhausted.
\\

\noindent It is important to stress that the final volumes resulting from the simulation model are directly dependent on the input data. Currently, the climate datasets simulate an extreme year that will likely never occur. The actual tank volumes and heat pump capacities required in real-life can be assumed to be much lower. Moreover, these volumes are based on active HVAC without the use of natural ventilation. Even though, the case study specifically required the thermal demand modelling without natural ventilation, in most other cases, the use of natural ventilation before active HVAC drastically reduces thermal demand, and therefore likely also reduces the required TES tank volume. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Synthesis and Research Conclusions} This research developed a comprehensive design-to-operation basis for the integration of Thermal Energy Storage (TES) systems. By replacing theoretical, static sizing methods with a dynamic, constraint-aware methodology and simulation model, this study provides a thermal buffering solution to mitigate electrical grid congestion. 

\subsubsection{Research Objectives} The core research objective was to develop a unified framework that optimizes thermal storage through transient energy calculations and precise energy deficit tracking. This was achieved by integrating a multi-zone thermal network demand model with a simulation model that utilises advanced control strategies. 
\\

\noindent The study demonstrates that TES implementation is not a purely thermal challenge but a multi-dimensional optimization problem. The developed workflow successfully transforms building energy profiles into manageable, grid-aware loads by identifying the non-linear relationship between heating capacity, cooling hydraulic stability, control strategies, and storage volume requirements.

\subsubsection{Main Findings} The main finding are summarized below and answer the main research question and its sub-questions.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Varying Load Profiles}: Suitable TES technology selection is dependent on a building's load profile, where the Chronological Hourly Load curve gives most insight into its thermal behaviour.
    \item \textbf{TES Integration}: Important infrastructure and system-level requirements for integrating TES into building climate systems is choosing a TES technology, TES medium, and operating temperatures that are compatible with the heat source and emitters.
    \item \textbf{Systemic Buffering}: This study confirms that combining transient thermal network modelling with active control strategies provides a critical thermal buffer, allowing the system to resolve thermal comfort violations that baseline configurations cannot address. This dynamic modelling for a continuous energy balance provides a more robust and efficient TES volume compared to simple static calculations.
       \item \textbf{Constraint-Aware Design}: Storage footprint is governed not only by thermal deficits but by structural hydraulic stability and peak power availability. The case study illustrates that designing for thermal capacity alone is insufficient, as systems often reach saturation points where further hardware investment yields negligible efficiency gains.
    \item \textbf{Framework Applicability}: The framework and its elements has proven to be able to provide a solid basis for TES selection, integration, and operation. However, as the case study has shown, TES implementation is very case specific, where every TES technology, medium, and heat source are major influential factors on a systemic level. 
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Flexibility and Adaptability} The entire simulation model architecture allows for personalized and transferable inputs, ensuring the framework remains adaptable rather than rigid. Users can define specific building characteristics and control strategies as variables within the simulation. While the architecture is primarily energy-based, it incorporates some essential hydraulic constraints, providing a versatile tool that can be tailored to various architectural configurations and operational requirements.

