\section{Limitations and Recommendations} 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Limitations} This research acknowledges several constraints that affect the scalability and precision of the findings. Since this thesis is comprised of two parts: the general TES framework and the case study, the limitations for each part will be addressed.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection{General TES Framework} The broader applicability of this research is constrained by a primary focus on energy management. This thesis offers a generalized understanding of Thermal Energy Storage utility, yet it does not account for the extensive variability in specific system designs. The case study does include a transient thermal analysis but is mostly based on sensible heat storage and not other forms of TES. 
\\

\noindent As each system presents unique inherent challenges, the general TES framework approaches energy treatment from a more macro-management perspective rather than exploring the internal fluid dynamics or local thermodynamic processes. Therefore, actual integration, design, and control strategies will vary significantly depending on the defined system boundaries. 
\\

\noindent Lastly, the developed framework is inherently biased toward sensible storage, particularly buffer tanks. Even though the general TES framework is kept as broad as possible, the implementation logic is partly derived from the operational characteristics of sensible TES.
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Case Study} 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection*{Single-node Energy Bucket} Concerning the storage medium, the buffer tank is modelled as a single, perfectly mixed node. By failing to track temperature stratification layers within the volume, the model loses the  ability to account for the actual state of charge gradients. Therefore the real exergy inside the tank is not precisely known. Not as accurate, but a way to account for this lack of information, a total efficiency factor ($\epsilon$), comprised of a stratification efficiency factor and a dead zone volume, is applied. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection*{Atrium Air Temperature Gradient} Regarding the spatial modelling of the building, the atrium (zone 2) is presented as a single zone with a uniform temperature. The development of thermal stratification temperature layers is neglected. In reality, large vertical spaces typically exhibit significant temperature layering due to buoyancy effects. 
\\

\noindent Originally, the UFH system was designed to only heat or cool the lower 3 meters for occupants, and leave the upper air temperatures free-floating within the set temperature boundaries. However, the simulation model explores the UFH system as an active TES system pre-charging the atrium as a buffer for the cascaded air strategy as well as internal heat exchange between zones. Moreover, modelling thermal stratification within the atrium would necessitate live-tracking of temperature gradients at each time-step of the simulation. Because the zones receiving cascaded air are located at varying elevation (floors), their supply air temperature is fundamentally dependent on the atrium's local air temperature at that specific height. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection*{Static Heat Exchange Coupling) The simulation of heat loss physics maintains a static boundary condition that does not fully respond to control actions. When the buffer script applies a temperature swing to the system, the external heat exchange and coupling losses are not dynamically updated to reflect the new temperature differential with the outside temperature. Consequently, the model misses the feedback loop where a higher or lower operating temperature would alter the rate of thermal leakage.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection*{Emitter Dynamics and Thermal Inertia} A further limitation of the model lies in the assumed thermal coupling between the emitters and the room air. The simulation calculates temperature drift based on the global thermal mass of the building's construction. However, it implicitly assumes that air temperature follows the same cooling curve of this thermal mass. 
\\

\noindent In reality, convective air-based systems (AHUs, VAV units, FCUs) have negligible thermal inertia. This means that when these systems are deactivated, the indoor air temperature often experiences a rapid decline due to infiltration and convection, even while the structural mass remains warm. By failing to differentiate between the rapid thermal decay of air-supplied zones and the sustained radiant output of structural mass, the model may overestimate the effective temperature drift duration of the indoor environment. This would lead to an overly optimistic assessment of energy savings achievable through the allowed temperature swing in the control strategy. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection*{Heat Pump Configuration} Finally, this model assumes a dedicated heat pump for both the hot and cold storage tanks, with each unit operating exclusively within its assigned circuit. However, in reality, many systems utilize heat pumps in a parallel configuration, allowing for greater operational flexibility. In such configurations, heat pumps are not strictly bound to a single tank, but instead they can be dynamically reassigned to either heating or cooling based on real-time demand.
\\

\noindent By treating the capacity as a fixed, isolated constraint, the model may underestimate the system's true potential. In reality, both units could theoretically switch heating or cooling mode to address a surge in demand in the opposing circuit. This would effectively sacrifice the performance of their "dedicated" tank to mitigate a more critical deficit elsewhere. Failing to account for this parallel flexibility, where the heat pumps can work together to prioritize the highest thermal demand, results in a conservative estimation of the system's maximum output. This may limit the model's ability to accurately represent how real-world systems respond to grid congestion or significant shifts in peak demand.
\\

\noindent Moreover, if both heat pumps enter the same mode, supplying the same tank with energy, instead of one tank operating at max capacity and the other heat pump supplying only the extra additional required energy, they can spread the thermal load equally. This would lead to both heat pumps running at a lower capacity which is more efficient and less mechanical stress on the systems. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Recommendations} Future research would mainly aim to advance the robustness and applicability of the proposed methodology by directly addressing the limitations identified in both the general TES framework and the case study. The following recommendations are structured to bridge the gap between global energy management and high-fidelity physical modelling. This would ensure that future iterations improve upon current simplifications regarding system dynamics, thermal coupling, and operational control. By targeting these current constraints, the following recommendations provide a path toward refining the accuracy and real-world application possibilities of the thermal energy storage implementation framework. 

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Live-link Between Simulations} An improvement for accuracy in the simulation models would be transitioning from the current sequential, CSV-based data exchange to a high-fidelity, bidirectional coupling between the building performance model (multi-zone model) and the thermal storage controller (TES Simulation Engine). In the current workflow, the TES model operates on pre-calculated, fixed load profiles, preventing the system from accounting for the transient interactions between building physics and control actions. By establishing a live, real-time link, the simulation can accurately capture the net thermal demand. 
\\

\noindent This live-link would be able to dynamically update heat losses as a result of the allowed temperature drifts inside the Simulation Engine. Moreover, climate strategies like: natural ventilation, active sun shading, and free-floating thermal boundaries would be possible inside the multi-zone model. This is not possible in the current architecture because the Simulation Engine requires a fixed reference set-point temperature for all time-steps, in order to prevent the double accounting of thermal mass usage. The case study specifically bases its HVAC sizing without these climate strategies, however, most other buildings would utilise them to reduce thermal demand.
\\

