\noindent This integration ensures the Simulation Engine possesses precise, step-by-step awareness of both the interior temperature and the active thermal load at the moment of HVAC engagement, thereby correcting for the inaccuracies inherent in static, decoupled estimations. The reason this was not done in the first place is because of time constraints and computational power, since this live-link would perform more calculations per time-step.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{System Energy Conversion and Operational Constraints} The current simulation uses a simple formula for heat transfer for its emitters, $Q_{delivered, i} = UA \cdot \Delta T_{i}$, where the temperature difference is just the gap between the incoming air and the target room temperature. It also uses a fixed $UA$ value of $0.5\text{ kW/K}$ for every zone. This approach is not fully accurate because it ignores the fact that different emitter units (like fan coils, VAV units, and AHUs) have their own unique specifications and performance limits. Furthermore, heat transfer is more realistically calculated using the Log Mean Temperature Difference ($\Delta T_{lm}$) rather than a basic temperature difference.
\\

\noindent To make the research more realistic, future models should replace these general assumptions with more exact system specs and calculations. By using the $\Delta T_{lm}$ method and applying specific $UA$ values for different pieces of equipment, the model can better reflect how these systems actually work. This allows for a much more accurate picture of how these emitters interact with changing air temperatures, giving a clearer understanding of how the equipment performs in real-world conditions. More accurate modelling will also reflect a more accurate (realistic) demand per time-step, since its a direct result of these systems.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Heat Pump COP} By transitioning from a theoretical Lorentz-based COP to performance-based COP curves, real-world heat pump operation and efficiency is achieved. The current approach relies on an idealized thermodynamic limit (although capped), which inherently overestimates efficiency by omitting the non-linear operational losses and part-load penalties experienced by real hardware. 
\\

\noindent Instead of relying on theoretical formulas to calculate heat pump efficiency, future models should use real-world performance data from manufacturers. Heat pumps don't run at perfect theoretical limits, since their efficiency changes depending on the workload and the weather. By using actual performance curves, the model can account for how the equipment really behaves, which gives a much more accurate picture of energy consumption and system performance.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Heat Pump Dual Mode} To move beyond the current assumption of isolated heat pump operation, future research should implement a parallel configuration where heat pumps are dynamically reassigned between hot and cold circuits based on real-time demand. This implementation can effectively mimic a dual-mode heat pump setup by allowing units to switch modes or collaborate to satisfy a high-priority thermal deficit. Specifically, when demand in one circuit becomes critical, the control logic should permit a heat pump to pivot from its dedicated tank to assist the other, effectively doubling the available thermal capacity. This increase in available thermal capacity will ultimately result in a smaller required TES tank, because the energy deficit will be smaller when the heat pump thermal supply is increased. This solution prevents the need for a higher rated capacity heat pump.
\\

\noindent To maintain model simplicity without requiring the complexity of modelling internal thermal stratification, this flexibility can be governed by a "drift penalty" rule. When a heat pump is reassigned to assist the opposing circuit, the tank left behind experiences a pre-defined energy reduction based on the duration of no supply/maintenance. This penalty represents the thermal degradation and temperature drift that occurs during the period of non-supply. This approach allows the framework to capture the operational benefits of load-sharing, such as increased total system output and improved efficiency from reduced part-load stress, without the need for high-fidelity fluid dynamic modelling.

\subsubsection{Advanced Optimization}
To build upon this research, future work could focus on refining control logic and expanding the simulation environment. Investigating advanced optimization methods, such as Genetic Algorithms, could identify the smallest storage volumes that standard linear programming might overlook, potentially revealing opportunities to reduce tank sizes without violating occupant thermal comfort. 
\\

\noindent Furthermore, testing alternative control architectures like Reinforcement Learning alongside higher-fidelity building physics would allow for a more rigorous assessment of system resilience. This would help determine whether a smaller physical storage footprint can remain reliable during peak demand periods without compromising performance.

