\section{Appendix - Transient First-order Lumped Capacitance Model} The multi-zone model is this thesis is developed by building upon the foundational principles of the simplified, one-zone thermal model detailed in the provided documentation. The core methodology utilizes a one-node approach, where the combined thermal storage capacity of the building-including air volume, furniture, and internal components-is represented by a single node to estimate the indoor temperature. 
\\

\noindent By transitioning from this baseline one-zone framework to a multi-zone configuration, the model facilitates a more in-depth analysis of thermal interactions between zones while maintaining the rigorous thermodynamic balance of energy flows, such as transmission, ventilation, infiltration, and internal heat gains. This approach allows for a structured, physics-based assessment of building performance, ensuring that design choices are evaluated against realistic transient conditions.
\\

\subsection{Theoretical Background} The following methodology is property of the TU Delft and is created by one of the supervisors of this thesis: Willem van der Spoel, head of department of building physical at the Faculty of Architecture and the Built Environment.
\\

\noindent Determining the heating and cooling requirements for a building involves an inventory of all sensible heat flows. While latent heat can be significant, this model focuses exclusively on sensible heat. The system is defined as a building or room bounded by the external surfaces of the walls, roof, and ground floor. The energy balance considers six primary heat flows: transmission, ventilation, infiltration, solar gains, internal gains, and thermal accumulation.
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Heat Balance Components}
\\

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Transmission}: Heat transfer through external envelopes due to temperature gradients.
\\

\begin{equation}
Q_{tr,e}(t) = U_e A (T_e(t) - T_i(t))
\end{equation}
\\

For the ground floor, heat flow is estimated using a constant ground temperature ($T_{gr}$):
\\

\begin{equation}
Q_{tr,gr}(t) = U_{gr} A (T_{gr}(t) - T_i(t))
\end{equation}
\\

\item \textbf{Ventilation and Infiltration}: Sensible heat exchange from intentional air supply and unintentional leakage.
\\

\begin{equation}
Q_{vent}(t) = (1-\eta) \dot{m}_{vent} c_a (T_e(t) - T_i(t))
\end{equation}
\\

\begin{equation}
Q_{inf}(t) = \dot{m}_{inf} c_a (T_e(t) - T_i(t))
\end{equation}
\\

\item \textbf{Solar Gains}: Radiation enters through glazing and is absorbed by internal mass.
\\

\begin{equation}
Q_{i,sol,gl}(t) = g_{gl} g_{sh} A_{gl} q_{sol,e}(t)
\end{equation}
\\

For opaque surfaces, solar absorption increases the surface temperature, resulting in transmitted heat:
\\

\begin{equation}
Q_{i,sol,op}(t) = U_c A \frac{a_{sol} q_{sol}(t)}{\alpha_e}
\end{equation}
\\

\item \textbf{Internal Gains}: Heat generated by occupants (approx. 65 W/pp), appliances, and lighting.
\end{itemize}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Thermal Storage and Balance} A one-node model represents the thermal mass ($M$) of the room, assuming all internal capacity is concentrated at a single operative temperature ($T_i$). The balance equation is defined as:
\\

\begin{equation}
M \frac{dT_i(t)}{dt} + Q_e(t) + Q_i(t) + Q_{h/c}(t) = 0
\end{equation}
\\

Where $M$ is the sum of component masses and specific heat capacities:
\\

\begin{equation}
M = \sum m_n c_n
\end{equation}
\\

To simplify, heat flows dependent on external temperatures are bundled into a single term ($H_e$):
\\

\begin{equation}
H_e = \sum U_e A + (1-\eta)\dot{m}{vent} c_a + \dot{m}{inf} c_a
\end{equation}
\\

\noindent This one-node simplification allows for transient temperature calculations by assuming environmental conditions remain constant over a discrete time-step $\Delta t$, enabling the derivation of indoor temperature development over time.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Transient Temperature Calculation}
The heat balance is solved as a differential equation to determine the indoor temperature development over time. By assuming that external conditions ($T_e$ and $T_{gr}$) remain constant over a discrete time-step $\Delta t$, the governing equation becomes:
\\

\begin{equation}
M \frac{dT_i(t')}{dt'} + (H_e + H_{gr})T_i(t') = Q_{total}(t)
\end{equation}
\\

Where $Q_{total}(t)$ aggregates internal gains ($Q_i$) and heating/cooling power ($Q_{h/c}$). The exact solution for the indoor temperature at the end of the time-step ($t + \Delta t$) is:
\\

\begin{equation}
T_i(t + \Delta t) = T_{i,eq} + (T_i(t) - T_{i,eq}) \cdot \exp\left( -\frac{H_e + H_{gr}}{M} \Delta t \right)
\end{equation}
\\

In this expression, $T_{i,eq}$ represents the steady-state equilibrium temperature that the zone would reach as $t \to \infty$:
\\

\begin{equation}
T_{i,eq} = \frac{Q_i + Q_{h/c} + H_e T_e + H_{gr} T_{gr}}{H_e + H_{gr}}
\end{equation}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsubsection{Thermostat Control Logic} The simulation incorporates a control loop to maintain indoor temperatures between defined set-points ($T_{set,h}$ for heating and $T_{set,c}$ for cooling). Within each time-step, the model assesses three operational states to determine the required power ($Q_{h/c}$):
\
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Controlled response}: When the target temperature is reached within $\Delta t$, the system modulates output to maintain $T_{set}$, and power is derived by solving for $Q_{h/c}$ in the balance equation.
\item \textbf{Capacity constrained}: When the capacity is insufficient to reach $T_{set}$ within $\Delta t$, the unit operates at maximum output ($Q_{h/c,max}$), and the resulting temperature is calculated using the transient solution.
\item \textbf{Free-floating}: When the indoor temperature remains within the deadband ($T_{set,h} < T_i < T_{set,c}$), no active conditioning is required.
\end{enumerate}
\medskip

\noindent In the Excel tool (of the one-zone model), the following workflow is used at each time-step:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Pre-calculate inside temperature $T(t+\Delta t)$ for four situations:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[a.] No heating and no cooling with $H_{e,heat}$, denoted as $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,heat}$ (free floating)
        \item[b.] No heating and no cooling with $H_{e,cool}$, denoted as $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,cool}$ (free floating)
        \item[c.] Maximum heating capacity with $H_{e,heat}$, denoted as $T(t+\Delta t)_{heat}$
        \item[d.] Maximum cooling capacity with $H_{e,cool}$, denoted as $T(t+\Delta t)_{cool}$
    \end{itemize}

    \item The actual inside temperature $T(t+\Delta t)$ is chosen according to the following flow of logic:
\end{enumerate}
\\

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{quote}
\small
\texttt{If } $T(t+\Delta t)_{heat} < T_{set,h}$ \texttt{ then } $T(t+\Delta t) = T(t+\Delta t)_{heat}, Q_h = Q_{h,max}$ \texttt{ else} \\
\texttt{If } $T(t+\Delta t)_{heat} > T_{set,h}$ \texttt{ and } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,heat} < T_{set,h}$ \texttt{ then } $T(t+\Delta t) = T_{set,h}$ \texttt{ (calculate } $Q_h$\texttt{) else} \\
\texttt{If } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,heat} > T_{set,h}$ \texttt{ and } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,heat} < T_{set,v}$ \texttt{ then } $T(t+\Delta t) = T(t+\Delta t)_{free,heat}, Q_h = Q_c = 0$ \texttt{ else} \\
\texttt{If } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,heat} > T_{set,v}$ \texttt{ and } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,cool} < T_{set,v}$ \texttt{ then } $T(t+\Delta t) = T_{set,v}, Q_h = Q_c = 0$ \texttt{ else} \\
\texttt{If } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,cool} > T_{set,v}$ \texttt{ and } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,cool} < T_{set,c}$ \texttt{ then } $T(t+\Delta t) = T(t+\Delta t)_{free,cool}, Q_h = Q_c = 0$ \texttt{ else} \\
\texttt{If } $T(t+\Delta t)_{cool} < T_{set,c}$ \texttt{ and } $T(t+\Delta t)_{free,cool} > T_{set,c}$ \texttt{ then } $T(t+\Delta t) = T_{set,c}$ \texttt{ (calculate } $Q_c$\texttt{) else} \\
\texttt{If } $T(t+\Delta t)_{cool} > T_{set,c}$ \texttt{ then } $T(t+\Delta t) = T(t+\Delta t)_{cool}, Q_c = Q_{c,max}$
\end{quote}
\medskip

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\noindent The model performs a predictive check for each hour, evaluating four scenarios-free-floating heating, free-floating cooling, maximum heating, and maximum cooling—to select the appropriate state and ensure set-point compliance. This logic accounts for ventilative cooling via the window opening set-point ($T_{set,v}$), dynamically adjusting $H_e$ based on the operational mode.

