\section{Appendix - Multi-zone Model} This section will cover the complete Python code for the multi-zone thermal network model that was used in this thesis. This section will cover the complete Python code for the multi-zone thermal network model that was used in this thesis. This version does not include the Grid Search or MPC methodology, which are executed by an external master engine script and are considered outside the scope of this core model documentation. The code is divided into sections to get a clear overview.
\\

\noindent The ZoneModel class serves as the fundamental computational unit of the simulation, encapsulating the thermodynamic state and energy dynamics of an individual zone. The Orchestrator acts as the high-level manager, instantiating this class to create a scalable array of independent zone models. By maintaining a collection of these objects, the Orchestrator effectively decouples local zone physics from global system constraints.

\subsection{Zone Model Class} During each simulation time step, the Orchestrator sequences the execution of these models, passing aggregate boundary conditions (such as external temperature, solar gains, and shared HVAC supply) to each ZoneModel instance. It then collects the resulting thermal loads and indoor temperatures from every zone, computing the necessary system-level heating or cooling demand. This architecture allows the Orchestrator to solve the global energy balance while delegating the complex, zone-specific transient heat transfer calculations to the individual ZoneModel class.

\subsubsection{Zone Model Initialisation} The ZoneModel class serves as the localized engine for thermal simulation, encapsulating all physical and operational parameters required to compute the transient response of a single space. By utilizing a dictionary-based kwargs unpacking strategy, the orchestrator passes a comprehensive suite of inputs including geometry, thermal properties, internal gain profiles, and HVAC system constraints into each instance. During initialization, the model calculates derived constants such as total thermal capacity and initializes internal surface structures, ensuring that each zone maintains a consistent state while remaining flexible enough to represent diverse architectural configurations.
