\subsection{Main Simulation Loop} The simulation engine acts as the temporal orchestrator for the entire system, processing sequential thermal demand profiles across multiple zones. It employs a multi-strategy logic to manage thermal energy storage (TES) and heat pump dispatch concurrently for both heating and cooling circuits. At each time-step, the engine evaluates energy balances, prioritizes thermal comfort through either structural mass interaction or active TES discharge, and dynamically reallocates output based on infrastructure congestion and system efficiency constraints. This parallel execution ensures that heating and cooling demands are balanced against available thermal energy and grid availability.
